shared date-keyed filename; fixed by giving each mode its own output path | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] | Handoff with `memlint.py` and four supporting files left uncommitted by choice, a temp-path placement recurrence, and a cross-session \"another session may have fixed it\" check | 2026-09-02.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/always-loaded-context-is-the-expensive-place-to-be-wrong]] | Wrongness, staleness, and volume are three costs on the same session-resident surface; consolidation concentrates the first unless the pointer's target is re-verified | 2026-09-02.md, 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/microsoft-forms-vs-google-forms-inventory-mismatch]] | Live inventory survey runs on Microsoft Forms while project docs point at 08-13-era Google Forms; a `gws` query there returns a truthful-but-false \"no new responses\", exposed only by the raw `.xlsx` header row | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/tier-a-memory-as-the-durable-payload-of-a-verification-session]] | A verification check's payload is a trap plus a command, so it routes to Tier A memory rather than a handoff doc; reinforced by the project not being a git repo | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[connections/a-stale-pointer-resolves-cleanly-to-the-wrong-answer]] | Stale Google Form IDs, a stale DEV memory router, and a nearly-dangling AGENTS.md pointer all succeed at resolution while returning the wrong answer; check from the target's side and stamp a verification date | 2026-09-07.md | 2026-09-07 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] | Adds the unpushed `dc54c97`/`38e462b` commits and the cross-link to `DexaProject` still living as a public GitHub repo with a patient CSV | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] | Adds the 78→86 job-count drift from 2026-09-15, restating the tell in its threshold-free form (single-digit okCount = SSD absent) | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] | A `cp` to a date-keyed archive path destroyed three prior `.errors.log` lines (recovered from git); archive names must not collide or must append; `cp /dev/null` is the only working truncation route | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/session-transcript-tracking-repo-growth]] | Adds the 30-day auto-deletion retention motive and the same-day exclude-then-bulk-commit handling that resolves into a per-commit-intent rule | 2026-09-09.md, 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/the-preservation-step-must-itself-be-verified]] | Archive-before-clear and free-run-overwrites-paid both failed by encoding a once-daily assumption into a filename; the verified tarball discipline shows why an unchecked safety net is worse than none | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] | `~/DEV` converted to thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 router files; `/*` block-all `.gitignore` as the load-bearing line, `CLAUDE.md` deliberately kept as a workspace map, Windows box confirmed active | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] | 21 public repos read inside: only `B_Ellis` and `cmj-norms-app` held real athlete data; 404-as-live bug and a Vercel-not-Pages miss in the liveness check; 18 flipped private | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] | 36 Windows/Mac conflicts collapse to 4 (26 CRLF-only, 6 Mac-newer generated); \"Mac wins\" would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] | Repo names, conflict flags, and cleaned exports all over- and under-include at once; treat the cheap list as a work queue, classify by kind before resolving | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] | Revisions supersede, accumulating records diverge; the correct verb is merge or append, and a smaller newer copy is the tell | 2026-09-09.md | 2026-09-09 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] | ~200 `.from-*` conflicts traced to a Windows save command mangling encoding and prepending BOMs; proved on `portfolio.json` (9 updates matched, nothing lost), root cause fixed, 95 BOM files left alone | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-oom-kill-during-first-time-index]] | Backup OOM-killed during a 20-min first-time index of `dev-docs` (547 files); index stale not corrupt, two dead-PID locks cleared, CPU% rejected as a health signal | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/backup-manifest-symlink-false-alarm]] | `manifest-unmanaged:_DRIVE` was a symlink, not a missing repo; backup taught to skip symlinks, two real gaps added with a 0 lost / 0 changed / 2 added proof | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] | Four \"not a repo\" folders were parent-tracked, five \"missing\" repos were name mismatches, two `repo-diverged` errors had self-cleared; verify before `gh repo create` | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/git-submodule-branch-pinning-decision]] | `ZoteroInjestion` made a real submodule but pinned to `mac-snapshot-2026-09-09` rather than `main`; a snapshot pin decays silently, decision deferred | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] | Mangled sync saves, a symlink-blind manifest check, and a backup committing its own output: recurrence is the tell that the fix belongs in the generator | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[connections/a-killed-process-leaves-debris-that-blocks-the-retry]] | Dead-PID locks and orphaned indexers are not self-healing; a loud crash becomes an indefinitely quiet no-op unless the kill's leavings are enumerated | 2026-09-11.md | 2026-09-12 |\n| [[concepts/harness-agnostic-aios-plan]] | Multi-task Mac+Windows skills/backup plan: 42 own vs 19 third-party skills reframing, Task 4 inversion script deleted, Task 2 prune 306→208 as a reversible move, three open round-10 findings | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/gitignore-trailing-slash-misses-symlinks]] | A `.gitignore` pattern ending in `/` matches directories but not symlinks, so the rule silently no-ops and an automated backup re-adds the entries; plus `.gitignore` vs `.git/info/exclude` scope | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/backup-verification-by-restore]] | Restore test returned 57 of 61 skills, exposing a two-hop symlink chain into an unbacked-up `~/.agents/vendor-review/`; only an actual restore can measure coverage the backup's own records cannot | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/cross-provider-review-gate]] | A rate-limit-forced switch from Codex to Claude found the plan's biggest error; resume cannot cross providers, and a prior approval is stale after an amendment (5 new bugs in the amendment itself) | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/submodule-conversion-breaks-rsync-backup]] | Submodule conversion turned `.git` from directory to file; rsync cannot overwrite a destination directory with a file, failing only while the SSD is mounted; fixed by rename, `okCount: 80` | 2026-09-12.md | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/a-type-change-at-the-source-breaks-the-mirror]] | Adds the Codex-skills symlink refusal as a second rsync type-change instance, making recurrence the argument for fixing the tooling | 2026-09-12.md, 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[connections/change-the-checker-down-or-sideways]] | Replacing a failed check with a primitive substrate works only where a ground truth exists; otherwise the only lever is a peer with uncorrelated blind spots. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/the-execution-context-silently-edits-the-work-set]] | Session lifetime and mount state both silently shrink what a run attempts without producing an error, and both are fixed by moving the verdict onto a durable artifact that publishes cardinality. | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[connections/test-the-layer-before-blaming-the-artifact]] | A failed upload or render usually indicts the transport layer, not the file — hold the artifact fixed and remove a stage before rebuilding anything | adjudicated | 2026-09-13 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] | Windows laptop had no working backup: ~15 GB single-copy, nine Disabled tasks, two triggerless ones, and the Mac's manifest installed as Windows'; `/MIR`→`/E` fix | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] | Four Task Scheduler gotchas: `schtasks /Change` can't add a schedule, triggerless tasks show `Ready` forever, `G:` is per-user so SYSTEM writes nothing, sleeping laptops miss fixed-time triggers | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] | Adds the 2026-09-21 instance where *verification* was handed over as one command with a pre-stated expected output | 2026-09-14.md, 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] | A received recovery report's findings held but one explanation didn't: `dev-sync` skips an enumerated list, not underscore-prefixed folders, so `_INBOX` was already a clean pushed repo | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] | Three Windows pushes mid-session each needing a merge, including a deliberate-looking 913-file skill audit cutting ~160→63; accepted because git retains deletions, intent still to be confirmed | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] | `Ready`, `status: OK`, and a backup's own records were each locally truthful while coverage failed; narrow stage predicates don't compose into end-to-end assurance | 2026-09-14.md | 2026-09-14 |\n| [[concepts/rsync-symlink-conversion-stale-directory]] | Codex skills converted to symlinks while the SSD held real directories; rsync cannot replace a directory with a symlink, fixed by moving four folders to `_OLD_codex-skills-dirs-2026-09-15/` | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/manifest-classification-not-inferable-from-names]] | Six Baylor-sounding repos (`VALD_Performance`, `RTP_ProgressReport`, …) were personal, not work; manifest classification must be asked, not inferred | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] | `.errors.log` archived and cleared only after a verified 0-error/86-job re-run — fixes are not their own confirmation | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/vendored-copy-drift-within-a-repo]] | `LibraryProject`'s embedded `ZoteroInjestion` one commit behind the standalone repo; `STATUS.md` missing its Next section with an unmerged Windows copy pasted at the bottom | 2026-09-15.md | 2026-09-16 |\n| [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] | Adds the 2026-09-21 cutover session: Windows-side three-line verification, the no-op 8 PM Windows task, and the pmset wake recommended then declined | 2026-09-21.md, 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]] | `load_snapshot.py` silently deleted 12,750 Mac-only 2024–2025 rows because a 2026-only check passed; rewritten test-first to merge by primary key, restored from its own pre-load backup | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/claude-code-stack-config-audit-2026-09-22]] | 50-session + 303-session audit: 5 zero-use plugins proposed for disabling, `: \"` in YAML breaking a whole SKILL.md header, dcg hook dead on Windows (913 failures, Mac path), skill-router 68 s worst case | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] | Adds the 2026-09-16 hand resolution as a third independent confirmation of the union-merge strategy, arriving before the merge driver was configured | 2026-09-16.md, 2026-09-22.md, 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/verify-across-every-partition-the-write-touched]] | A 2026-only row check, an exit-code-only pull check, and the three-check inventory counter-example: verification scope must span every dimension the operation could change | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/a-guard-that-cannot-run-fails-open]] | The dead Windows dcg hook and the never-fired Perch/VALD alerts both look identical to success; protective mechanisms need a liveness signal and a deliberate periodic trip | 2026-09-22.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] | Retired steps left in `refresh_all_data.bat` as greppable `skipped` markers make a cutover checkable by expected line count (3); the still-registered 8 PM no-op task is the residual reporting hazard | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/deferred-credential-rotation-recorded-rationale]] | Exposed VALD client secret deliberately not rotated (private repo, pulls work) and recorded as \"skip for now\"; a defensible deferral missing the falsifiable revisit trigger its sibling had | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look]] | One handoff plus four per-repo `STATUS.md` records: placement follows how the next reader arrives at the question, and deferrals need the record more than completed work does | 2026-09-21.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] | `repo-behind-blocked:global-memory` failed two nights because the backup never auto-merges; pull then a clean 21:12 run, plus persistent-vs-transient push failure triage | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/mac-evening-machine-schedule-split]] | Mac evening-machine role reaches the schedulers: rituals disabled (persists across reboot), backup 09:00→21:00 with `.bak-2026-09-24`, no-installer check before the hand edit, CRASHBOX prerequisite moved | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work]] | Adds the 2026-09-19 `graphify-out` instance — an automated daily job topping the recency ranking five days before the sync-commit case | 2026-09-19.md, 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/check-for-a-generator-before-hand-editing]] | Asking who writes a config file decides the shape of the fix; the absence of a generator is what makes a hand edit durable, the inverse of fix-the-generator | 2026-09-24.md | 2026-09-24 |\n| [[connections/false-alarms-are-fixed-at-the-check-not-the-threshold]] | Chronic alarms get fixed at the check or by re-siting the work; threshold tuning is legitimate only after one of those changed what the alarm means. | adjudicated | 2026-09-27 |\n| [[connections/the-second-reviewer-is-a-frame-not-an-extra-pass]] | A second review adds value in proportion to how differently framed it is, and relying on reviewer outages to supply that frame makes it arrive at random. | adjudicated | 2026-09-27 |\n| [[concepts/hook-command-quoting-home-expansion]] | Single-quoted `$HOME` in a hook command blocks expansion → exit 127 surfaced as a \"non-blocking status\" error; startup \"HOOK FAILURES\" lines are a different signal (scheduled jobs) | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/stale-git-lock-files-block-mass-pull]] | An interrupted overnight run left 138 empty `.git/*.lock` files silently blocking all pulls; moved not deleted as `*.lock.stale-2026091x` | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]] | `Claude outputs` backup failure resolved by moving two postgame `.docx` to Drive per the season README rather than widening the manifest; Drive tree reads OOM-killed | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/one-artifact-two-storage-regimes-pick-one]] | `Claude outputs` (decided, moved) and session transcripts (undecided, growing) show that extending coverage from the wrong home is cheap once and expensive forever | 2026-09-16.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/git-fetch-before-declaring-clean]] | `/dev-sync` called ~140 repos clean without ever fetching; fetching revealed 5 behind — recorded as the skill's single biggest failure mode | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/daily-backup-push-error-miscount]] | `PUSH ERR \\| Everything up-to-date` — git's no-op success message counted as an error, plus every log line written twice | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/never-trim-a-memory-line-without-checking-for-a-second-copy]] | A compress attempt at 2,496/2,500 chars nearly destroyed a fact's only copy; move to the Tier A slate and replicate cross-machine instead | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[concepts/concurrent-claude-sessions-same-repos]] | Two Claude sessions on the same repos: bad-grep-looks-like-revert, per-session handoffs, a redundant `Linda_Projects` clone, real `global-memory` conflicts | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n| [[connections/a-checks-silence-is-not-a-negative-result]] | An un-fetched clean verdict and a mis-quoted grep both return reassuring silence because the check could not speak; capability belongs in the output | 2026-09-19.md | 2026-09-29 |\n\n\n## Related Wiki Articles\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\"\naliases: [union merge transcripts, TOS_Evidence_Brief missing from manifest, 146 repos walked, Linda Projects space breaks sync step, Tier A does not cross machines, backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22, 22 active 117 dormant, manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom, delete only the conflict markers, keep both sides no from-files]\ntags: [git, sync, backup, macos, memory, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-16.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-24.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-09-29\n---\n\n# DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\n\nA `/dev-sync` run on 2026-09-22 walked 146 repositories to bring the Mac in line with GitHub: two pulled (`_INBOX`, `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`), one pushed (`CommandCentre`), one cloned (`TOS_Evidence_Brief`, created on Windows 9/21), with 22 active and 117 dormant-and-clean, and no conflicts. Its durable findings concern how recurring cross-machine friction is resolved: a merge strategy for append-only logs, a manifest that does not discover its own new inputs, and the fact that Tier A memory does not travel between machines. The transcript-merge finding was already established on 2026-09-16 and recurred again on 2026-09-24, making it a three-instance pattern.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Union merge is the right strategy for append-only transcript logs.** Mac/Windows conflicts in `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` resolve cleanly by keeping every line from both machines, producing no `.from-*` files to clean up afterward.\n- **The same resolution was reached independently on 2026-09-16**, where `global-memory` transcript-log conflicts were settled by keeping both sides rather than picking a winner, again with no `.from-*` files created — before the merge strategy was configured.\n- **The manual form is to delete only the conflict markers.** On 2026-09-24 two clashing transcript logs (9/23 and 9/24) were resolved exactly this way — text from both sides kept, only the `<<<<<<<`/`=======`/`>>>>>>>` lines removed — confirming that for this artifact class there is nothing to adjudicate.\n- **The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos.** `TOS_Evidence_Brief` — created on Windows the previous day — was absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it fixed the report (prior file at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`). On 2026-09-24 the identical class reappeared as `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom`.\n- **Tier A memory does not cross machines.** Durable GPS decisions were therefore written into the repo's own `docs/STATUS.md` *and* `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so the Windows machine would see them.\n- **A folder name containing a space (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke a sync step again** and had to be redone with the path quoted — a recurrence of the same hazard seen on 2026-09-11.\n- **`compile.py` was deliberately not run.** A compile costs ~$1.28, so clearing the memory-compiler backlog was left as the user's call; the warning persists in `.errors.log` until then, and had grown to 3 logs behind by 2026-09-24.\n\n## Details\n\nThe union-merge finding names a resolution strategy that fits the artifact's semantics rather than a generic \"take one side\" rule. A transcript log is append-only and each machine appends independently, so the two versions are not competing revisions of the same content — they are disjoint additions to a shared record. Under that structure the correct merge keeps every line, and the usual conflict-file machinery (`.from-*` copies awaiting manual adjudication) is pure overhead because there is nothing to adjudicate. The pattern now has three independent confirmations arriving by different routes: a hand resolution on 9/16 that consciously declined to pick a winner, a configured merge driver on 9/22, and a purely mechanical marker-strip on 9/24. That an unaided human judgment, a configured strategy, and a mechanical cleanup all converge on identical output is the strongest available evidence that the strategy matches the artifact rather than the tooling — and it argues that the merge driver should have been configured after the first instance rather than the second. This is the same classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline applied in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where treating accumulating records as revisions would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows, and the accumulate-versus-supersede distinction generalized in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]. Notably, the 9/22 PFF loader bug was the identical error one layer down — a replace where a merge was required.\n\nThe manifest gap is a recurrence rather than a novelty, and its recurrence is the point. A repo created on one machine does not announce itself to a backup manifest maintained on another; the manifest's coverage is declared, not discovered, so a new repo is invisible to it until a human adds a line. The 09:05 backup error was in this case an honest signal — the manifest check did fire — but the general hazard is the one catalogued in [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] and [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]]: every managed item succeeds while the unmanaged item is never counted. Prior instances at [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] and [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]], the `Claude outputs` complaint resolved on 9/16 by relocation rather than by a manifest entry ([[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]]), and the `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` entry two days after this run, put the count well past the threshold at which — by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — the fix belongs in the discovery mechanism rather than in yet another manual manifest edit. Each individual fix is a one-line change, which is precisely why the structural fix keeps getting deferred.\n\nThe Tier A observation is a routing constraint with immediate practical force. Tier A memory is local to the machine that wrote it, so any decision that a second machine must honor — here, that the Mac owns the pulls and the Windows GPS writer is stopped — cannot live there alone. Writing it into the repository's `docs/STATUS.md` puts it in a location git already replicates, and mirroring it into `global-memory/MEMORY.md` reaches the synced memory surface; between them the decision arrives on the other machine by two independent paths. This extends the content-to-tier routing matrix in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] with a cross-machine axis, and matches the working-machine-authors-the-fix pattern in [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]]. Finally, the deliberate refusal to run `compile.py` is a recorded non-action rather than an oversight: a ~$1.28 spend is a user decision, so the backlog warning was left standing in `.errors.log` with the command documented in the compiler's README — consistent with treating `.errors.log` as an escalation channel that clears only on a verified clean run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior conflict triage that established classifying by kind before resolving; union merge is the resolution for the accumulating-record kind.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why an append-only log must be merged rather than overwritten.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — An earlier instance of the same declared-not-discovered manifest gap.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard designed against exactly this omission.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]] — The 2026-09-16 manifest complaint resolved by relocating the folder instead of widening coverage.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: an unlisted item cannot fail a check it was never part of.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 sync where `Linda Projects`' embedded space first broke the parallel fetch loop.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier-routing matrix this session extends with a cross-machine constraint.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The backlog warning deliberately left in `.errors.log`, at 3 logs behind by 2026-09-24.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the warning stays until a verified clean compile, not until the fix is merely intended.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The GPS decisions whose cross-machine visibility motivated the dual write.\n- [[concepts/mac-evening-machine-schedule-split]] — The 2026-09-24 session in which both the union-merge and manifest-gap findings recurred.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — The recurrence argument for moving the manifest fix into discovery.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-16.md]] — End-of-day wrap session (backfilled 2026-09-19 12:24): `global-memory` transcript-log conflicts resolved by keeping both sides rather than picking a winner, with no `.from-*` files created — the first of three instances of the union-merge resolution.\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Sync and closeout session (17:25): `/dev-sync` walking 146 repos with `_INBOX` and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` pulled, `CommandCentre` pushed, `TOS_Evidence_Brief` cloned, 22 active / 117 dormant / no conflicts; union merge resolving Mac/Windows transcript-log conflicts without `.from-*` files; `TOS_Evidence_Brief` missing from `backup-manifest.json` causing the 09:05 backup error, fixed with a `.bak-2026-09-22` backup; `DEV/Linda Projects` space breaking a sync step; Tier A memory not crossing machines, resolved by writing to `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md`; `compile.py` (~$1.28) deliberately left as the user's call.\n- [[daily/2026-09-24.md]] — Evening KeystoneOS closeout (21:25): two clashing transcript logs (9/23, 9/24) resolved by keeping text from both sides and deleting only the conflict markers; `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` reported by the backup log and queued as an action item; memory compiler 3 logs behind with a manual `compile.py` run still outstanding.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Commit Recency Is Not Work Recency: Sync Commits Mask the Active Project\"\naliases: [newest commit is a sync commit, CFB later commits were sync only, 13:20 merge group, picking the active project, commit timestamp is not a work signal, classify the commit before ranking it, graphify-out daily job commits, exclude automated repos from pick-up ranking]\ntags: [git, workflow, verification, project-management, gotcha, automation]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-24.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-24\nupdated: 2026-09-29\n---\n\n# Commit Recency Is Not Work Recency: Sync Commits Mask the Active Project\n\nChoosing which project to work on during the 2026-09-24 evening session started from the obvious signal — the most recent commit across repositories — and that signal was wrong. `CFB_Victory_Model` carried the latest timestamps, but its commits were sync commits produced by automation and cross-machine reconciliation, not authored work. The genuinely newest real work was a 13:20 merge group in KeystoneOS, which was selected instead. The same trap had already been hit five days earlier, on 2026-09-19, when `graphify-out` topped the recency ranking on the strength of an automated daily job and had to be excluded before `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` could be identified as the real pick-up repo. A commit timestamp records when the repository was written to, which is a strictly weaker claim than when a human last worked on it.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Automation authors commits.** Nightly backups that commit working trees, sync jobs that push reconciled state, scheduled generation jobs, and merge commits from cross-machine pulls all advance the timestamp without representing any human decision.\n- **This inflates exactly the repos with the most machinery around them,** so the repositories with the best automation look the most active — an inversion of the signal's intent. `graphify-out` is generated output with a daily job; `CFB_Victory_Model` is heavily synced.\n- **The fix is to classify the commit before ranking it:** read the message and the diff, and ask whether a human authored the change. A *group* of related commits at one time (the 13:20 merge group) reads as a work session; a lone `sync`, backup, or generation commit does not.\n- **The same distinction resolves cross-machine conflicts,** where the question is likewise whether a change is authored content or mechanical churn rather than simply which copy is newer.\n- **A wrong answer here costs a whole session's direction,** not a single file, because it determines which project gets picked up and which handoff gets read.\n- **Generated output still gets committed.** On 2026-09-19 `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files were committed rather than left dirty — excluding a repo from the *ranking* is separate from leaving its working tree unclean.\n\n## Details\n\nThe underlying error is treating a proxy as the property it stands in for. \"Most recently committed\" is a genuinely useful heuristic for \"most recently worked on\" in a repository that only a human writes to, and it degrades exactly in proportion to how much automated writing happens. In this stack the automated write volume is substantial: the nightly backup commits and pushes working trees, `dev-sync` pulls and merges across the Windows and Mac machines, union merges append to shared logs, and a daily job regenerates `graphify-out` — each producing commits whose timestamps are recent and whose content is nobody's work. The most-automated repository therefore floats to the top of a recency ranking, which is precisely backwards from the intent of the query. The `repo-behind-blocked` failure on 9/24 is a reminder of how much of this traffic there is: the backup's ordinary mode of operation is to author commits in repositories the human has not touched.\n\nThat the same mistake was made twice within a week, on two different repositories and for two different automation causes, is the argument that the heuristic needs replacing rather than remembering. On 9/19 the tell was known in advance — the session explicitly noted that `graphify-out`'s newer commits came from an automated daily job — and the exclusion still had to be made by hand; on 9/24 the same reasoning had to be re-derived for sync commits. A rule applied manually at each use is a rule that will eventually be skipped, and it will be skipped on the evening when the operator is least inclined to read commit messages.\n\nThe remedy is the classify-before-resolving discipline that has repeatedly paid off elsewhere in this system. [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] collapsed thirty-six flagged conflicts to four by asking what *kind* of difference each one was before adjudicating any of them, and [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] generalizes the lesson that a later timestamp says nothing about content when the two versions are not revisions of one another. Applied to commit history, the classification is between authored and mechanical commits, and the cheap tell is shape rather than content: a cluster of commits within a short window, touching related files, with human-written messages, is a work session; an isolated commit with a generated message is not.\n\nA practical corollary is that automated commits should be made identifiable so this classification is mechanical rather than a judgement call each time. Consistent message prefixes for backup, sync, and generation commits, or a distinct committer identity, would let a recency query filter them out directly — the same reasoning that makes greppable `skipped` markers in [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] turn a cutover into a checkable line count. Absent such a convention, every \"what was I last working on?\" question requires reading the commits, and the risk is that the reading is skipped precisely on the evenings when the answer matters most.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — The parent lesson that a later timestamp carries no claim about content.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline, applied there to conflict files and here to commits.\n- [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] — The same session's evidence of how much commit traffic automation generates.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — The sync machinery whose merges and union merges produce recent, non-authored commits.\n- [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] — Unattended pushes from the other machine arriving mid-session as further non-authored history.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — A neighbouring git indicator whose narrow predicate is read as a broader operational fact.\n- [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] — The precedent for making a mechanical marker greppable so a check becomes automatic.\n- [[concepts/git-fetch-before-declaring-clean]] — The other 2026-09-19 sync finding, where the same skill's clean verdicts were unfounded.\n- [[concepts/graphify-incremental-merge]] — The graphify pipeline whose daily job produces the automated commits excluded from the 9/19 ranking.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-19.md]] — Morning `/dev-sync` session (09:17): `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` chosen as the pick-up repo on newest real commit, with `graphify-out` explicitly excluded because its newer commits come from an automated daily job rather than work; `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files committed rather than left dirty; recorded as the lesson that sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos over real work and that automation must be filtered before choosing a pick-up repo.\n- [[daily/2026-09-24.md]] — Evening KeystoneOS session (21:25): KeystoneOS selected as the active project because the 13:20 merge group was the newest real work while `CFB_Victory_Model`'s later commits were only sync commits; recorded as the lesson that a newer commit timestamp does not mean newer work and that sync commits masked the genuinely most-recent project.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Fix From the Working Machine, Hand the Broken One a Paste-Ready Page\"\naliases: [option A fix on Mac now, paste-ready Windows page, one paste instead of a debug job, instruction page not executable, backup-manifest.windows.json nothing reads it yet, one-command verification step]\ntags: [workflow, handoff, cross-machine, windows, macos, documentation, backup]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-21.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-14\nupdated: 2026-09-24\n---\n\n# Fix From the Working Machine, Hand the Broken One a Paste-Ready Page\n\nFacing a broken Windows backup that could only be *repaired* on Windows, the 2026-09-14 session chose to do as much of the work as possible from the Mac: edit the scripts, author the new manifest, and write a single instruction page for the Windows machine to follow. The stated rationale was that the edits are safe to make from the Mac, and doing so converts the eventual Windows session from a debugging job into one paste. The 2026-09-21 data-pull cutover applied the same split in its verification direction, reducing the Windows-side work to one command with an expected output.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The split is by what each machine can do, not by where the problem is.** File edits travel through the shared repos and were done on the Mac; only the operations that require the Windows machine's own scheduler (`Set-ScheduledTask`, admin PowerShell 7, a logged-in user session) were deferred.\n- **The deliverable is an instruction page, not an executable.** The user asked twice for clarification on \"the Windows file\" — the wording was ambiguous about whether it was a script to run or a page to follow. Naming the artifact's *kind* is part of the handoff.\n- **Verification can be handed over the same way.** On 2026-09-21 the Windows-side check was reduced to `git pull` plus `Select-String -Path refresh_all_data.bat -Pattern \"skipped\"` with an expected three lines — a one-paste task whose result the Mac-side session could interpret without seeing the machine.\n- **A file that nothing reads yet is documented as such.** `backup-manifest.windows.json` records the real Windows destinations, with an explicit note inside the file that no checker consumes it — it is documentation until a Windows checker exists.\n- **Two constraints were recorded as prohibitions:** do not move the scripts out of `DEV\\_INBOX\\` (the registered task points there), and do not re-enable the nine disabled tasks (their script is now the Mac's version).\n- **Deferring the fix to \"next time I'm on Windows\" was the rejected alternative,** because it would have carried the whole diagnosis into a future session that would have to re-derive it.\n\n## Details\n\nThe move generalizes beyond this incident. When two machines share a repository, the class of work that can be performed remotely is larger than it first appears: anything that is a file edit — scripts, configuration, manifests, instructions — can be authored from whichever machine the operator is currently sitting at, because the sync layer delivers it. What genuinely cannot be done remotely is narrow and identifiable: operations against the other machine's OS-level state, its scheduler, its mounted volumes, its running processes. Partitioning the work along that line front-loads all the thinking into the session that has the context and leaves the other machine a mechanical checklist. This is the same economy as the ten-section `docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md` queue in [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]], where the shared repo became a work channel between two machines that share no filesystem.\n\nThe 2026-09-21 cutover shows the pattern extending from repair to *confirmation*, which is the harder half. A remote verification step is only handable if its expected output is stated in advance; otherwise the receiving side must judge what it sees, which is the debugging work the pattern is trying to avoid. Marking the retired steps `skipped` rather than deleting them is what made that possible — it turned the question \"is the cutover complete on Windows?\" into a line count with a known answer of three (see [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]]). The general recipe is therefore: author the change from the working machine, and design the remote check so that it returns a value comparable against a number you have already written down.\n\nThe two documentation choices are what make the handoff durable rather than merely present. Writing \"nothing reads this yet\" *inside* `backup-manifest.windows.json` addresses the file's most likely future misreading — a maintainer finding a plausible manifest and assuming it is live, or finding it stale and assuming a checker broke. This is the same in-file rationale discipline that kept `CLAUDE.md` from being collapsed into an `@AGENTS.md` pointer, and that kept `DexaProject`'s permanent manifest warning from being repeatedly re-investigated: the explanation belongs in the file the next reader will open, not in a commit message. The two prohibitions serve the same function in reverse — they pre-empt the two most natural \"tidying\" impulses (move the scripts somewhere sensible; turn the disabled tasks back on), each of which would break something. The repeated clarification request about \"the Windows file\" is a small but real lesson about handoff wording: a path handed across a machine boundary is ambiguous between several kinds of artifact — a script to execute, a document to read, a config to install — and the receiving side cannot disambiguate from the filename.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-recovery-inventory]] — The failure this handoff was built to repair.\n- [[concepts/windows-backup-task-trigger-gotchas]] — The content of the paste-ready page.\n- [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] — The 2026-09-21 one-command verification and the marker discipline that made it checkable.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The cutover whose Windows-side work was reduced to a single paste.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — `docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md` as the established precedent for a shared repo carrying a cross-machine work queue.\n- [[concepts/handoff-document-placement-discipline]] — Handoffs belong in the project directory; here the destination is the *other* machine's project directory.\n- [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] — A prior handoff that likewise carried explicit deferrals and cross-session uncertainty.\n- [[concepts/machine-restore-closeout-handoff]] — Closeout classification into done / self-draining / parked, the same disposition applied here.\n- [[connections/document-the-mechanism-not-the-magic-number]] — Writing the reason into the artifact so the next reader inherits it.\n- [[connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look]] — The placement rule this handoff pattern depends on.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-14.md]] — Memory maintenance session (22:05): option A chosen — fix the scripts on the Mac now and hand Windows a paste-ready page — rather than waiting until next on Windows, on the rationale that the edits are safe from here and it turns the Windows session into one paste instead of a debug job; user asked twice for clarification that \"the Windows file\" is an instruction page to follow, not an executable; `backup-manifest.windows.json` created with an in-file note that nothing reads it yet; explicit prohibitions on moving the scripts out of `DEV\\_INBOX\\` and on re-enabling the nine disabled tasks.\n- [[daily/2026-09-21.md]] — Cutover and closeout session (18:30): the Windows-side verification step reduced to `git pull` in `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\_INBOX` plus `Select-String -Path refresh_all_data.bat -Pattern \"skipped\"` expecting three lines, confirmed done by Eric while all authoring happened on the Mac.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/google-workspace-cli-cleanup.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Google Workspace Cleanup via gws CLI (Drive Dedup + Gmail Organization)\"\naliases: [gws CLI, Google Workspace CLI, Drive duplicate deduplication, content-level diff dedup, Gmail receipts filter, gws OAuth unpublished app, Updates inbox filter, false-positive duplicate group]\ntags: [google-workspace, google-drive, gmail, file-management, cleanup, automation, oauth, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-25.md\"\ncreated: 2026-08-01\nupdated: 2026-08-01\n---\n\n# Google Workspace Cleanup via gws CLI (Drive Dedup + Gmail Organization)\n\nOn 2026-06-25, a Google Drive cleanup and Gmail inbox organization project was run through the Google Workspace CLI (`gws`), spanning duplicate-file deduplication, OAuth/auth-stability troubleshooting, Gmail signature setup, and inbox filtering for financial/receipt emails. The session established that duplicate detection by filename/metadata alone is unreliable — content-level diffing caught a false-positive duplicate group — and traced repeated OAuth session drops to an unpublished app.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Content-level verification before duplicate deletion:** filename/size heuristics are insufficient to confirm true duplicates. A \"group 180\" cover-letter set of three seemingly identical files contained one file with different mid-document text; content-level diffing caught the false positive. The same scrutiny was applied to video/audio \"duplicates\" flagged only by filename/size.\n- **No Drive files deleted without an explicit verification step:** the user manually verified a sample before approving deletion; the verified duplicate-deletion plan was only executed after user confirmation.\n- **OAuth session drops traced to an unpublished app:** repeated Google Workspace auth drops were root-caused to the `gws` app being unpublished. Publishing the app and confirming granted permissions resolved the recurring auth instability.\n- **Gmail organization:** the Updates inbox held 23,000+ messages; a dedicated label/filter was set up to auto-sort receipts/financial-transaction emails away from Updates. A Gmail signature was also configured.\n- **Working files consolidated** to a new folder at `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\agy` for easier access; the user deferred remaining email-cleanup recommendations to Claude's discretion.\n\n## Details\n\nThe governing principle of the session was conservatism around destructive operations, mirroring the safe-deletion discipline seen in other cleanup work. Rather than trusting the CLI's duplicate flags — which are derived from filename and metadata heuristics — the workflow inserted a mandatory content-verification step before any deletion. This caught a real false positive: a set of three cover-letter files that matched on name and size but differed in mid-document text. Had the deletion proceeded on heuristic matching alone, a genuinely distinct document would have been lost. The same content-level scrutiny was extended to video and audio files flagged as duplicates, where filename/size collisions are especially likely to be coincidental. This is a specific instance of the \"verify directly, don't trust the intermediate layer\" pattern: the duplicate-flag is an intermediate proxy for actual file identity, and it fails precisely when files share superficial metadata but differ in content.\n\nThe OAuth instability was a separate thread. The `gws` CLI's Google Workspace session was dropping repeatedly, interrupting the cleanup work. The root cause was that the underlying OAuth app was in an unpublished state, which imposes shorter token lifetimes and re-consent requirements; publishing the app and re-confirming the granted scopes stabilized the session. On the Gmail side, the Updates category had accumulated 23,000+ messages, obscuring actionable content; a dedicated label plus filter was created to route receipts and financial-transaction emails out of Updates automatically, and a signature was configured. The user consolidated saved working files into a new `DEV\\agy` folder and deferred the remaining Gmail loose-end recommendations to Claude's judgment, confirming \"all good for now\" with no outstanding follow-ups.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/google-drive-cleanup-antigravity]] — An earlier (2026-06-23) Google Drive cleanup run through the Antigravity CLI (`agy`); both sessions share the conservative \"verify before deleting\" duplicate-handling discipline and the intent to consolidate working files, but use different CLIs (gws vs. agy) and the gws session adds Gmail organization and an OAuth-publish fix.\n- [[connections/verify-before-trusting-tool-output]] — The content-level diff catching a false-positive duplicate group is a direct instance of stepping down to a lower-level, ground-truth check rather than trusting the CLI's filename/metadata-based duplicate flag.\n- [[concepts/accidental-git-init-home-directory]] — Shares the \"confirm the action is safe before a destructive operation\" discipline; both verify actual state before deleting rather than acting on a surface signal.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-25.md]] — Google Workspace cleanup session (13:42): Drive duplicate dedup with content-level verification, false-positive cover-letter group caught, video/audio duplicate scrutiny, OAuth-drop root cause (unpublished app) and fix, Gmail receipts label/filter and signature setup, `DEV\\agy` working-file consolidation.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\"\naliases: [49 unmanifested DEV folders, backup-manifest.json 78 to 125, job count 28 to 76, manifest coverage gap, AI_Analyst token-dashboard removed, DexaProject Google Drive warning, migration left folders unmanifested]\ntags: [backup, automation, macos, keystoneos, verification, maintenance, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Closing a 49-Folder Backup Manifest Coverage Gap\n\nOn 2026-09-09 a KeystoneOS daily backup run returned `status: ERROR` with two problems: the `~/.claude` repo was one commit behind GitHub (blocking its push) and 49 folders under `~/DEV` were absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`. Rather than triage the 49, the user confirmed that all of them mattered — the gap was a residue of an earlier migration — and every one was added. The manifest grew from 78 to 125 entries and the backup's job count went from 28 to 76.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gap was invisible.** Folders absent from the manifest are never backed up, and their absence produces no error that reads as urgent — only an advisory `manifest-unmanaged` class. The 49-folder hole had existed since a prior migration.\n- **All 49 were added, not triaged:** 47 as git repos (working-tree capture to the CRASHBOX SSD) and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs` — SSD copy only, no repo semantics).\n- **Two stale entries were removed** (`AI_Analyst`, `token-dashboard`) after confirming both had already moved into `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`, leaving 125 entries. Manifest hygiene runs in both directions: adding new folders and retiring moved ones.\n- **`DexaProject`'s warning was kept deliberately** — it lives on Google Drive intentionally, so its manifest complaint is an accepted permanent condition rather than a defect to silence. A separate later finding in the same day established that the *GitHub* copy of `DexaProject` still holds a patient CSV and must be deleted.\n- **The `~/.claude` behind-by-one state was resolved with `git pull --rebase --autostash`,** preserving two uncommitted edits (`settings.json`, `hooks/herdr-agent-state.sh`). Backups never auto-merge by design.\n- **Commits were made but not pushed** — `~/.claude` at `dc54c97` and `~/.claude/global-memory` at `38e462b` — with the push deliberately left to the next scheduled backup run.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the largest instance yet of the manifest-drift failure class first catalogued in [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] (three folders) and re-observed at [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] (five folders). The escalation from three to five to forty-nine is informative: the earlier gaps were ordinary drift from folders created faster than manifest entries, whereas this one had a single structural cause — a migration that moved or created many directories at once while the manifest, being hand-maintained, stayed where it was. Bulk moves are precisely the event a curated list cannot survive, which strengthens the standing proposal in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] to derive the manifest from a directory scan rather than maintaining it by hand. The default-deny guard did its job in that the folders were reported rather than silently excluded, but reporting alone is only sufficient when the report is acted on, and forty-nine entries accumulating across weeks shows the report being observed and deferred.\n\nThe removal of `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` is the neglected half of manifest maintenance. A manifest entry pointing at a folder that has moved into `_ARCHIVE` will either fail loudly (noise) or resolve to a stale path (worse), and neither outcome is caught by any check that only asks \"is every folder covered?\" The `DexaProject` decision demonstrates the third state a manifest entry can occupy: an accepted, documented exception whose warning is expected output rather than a pending task. Recording the reason — it lives on Google Drive intentionally — is what prevents a future maintenance pass from either \"fixing\" it or being confused by it, the same discipline applied to the two large graph files kept deliberately in the 2026-09-01 classification pass. Notably, the same day's repo-visibility audit found that this deliberate local exception coexists with a genuine problem at a different layer: `erash11/DexaProject` is still a GitHub repository containing a patient CSV, awaiting deletion. A documented exception on one axis does not clear the artifact on every axis.\n\nThe `git pull --rebase --autostash` choice is worth isolating as policy. The backup job pushes several repos, and a repo that is behind its remote cannot push; the tempting resolutions are a force-push (destroys remote history) or a merge (creates a commit the operator did not author). Rebasing with autostash preserves both the remote's history and the two uncommitted working-tree edits, and the stated principle — backups never auto-merge — keeps the backup system from making content decisions on the operator's behalf. This is the same restraint shown when a two-writer SecondBrain conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating it, per [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]]. The decision to commit `~/.claude` and `global-memory` without pushing follows the same logic in the other direction: the push is the backup's job, so leaving it to the next scheduled run keeps one owner for that action rather than racing it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed guard that surfaced the 49 folders; this run is the strongest argument yet for its auto-derivation follow-up.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-drift-and-launchd-status-lag]] — The original three-folder instance of the same failure class.\n- [[concepts/backup-error-log-recurring-failures-2026-08-16]] — The five-folder `manifest-unmanaged` instance between that one and this.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — The completeness metric this session reset from 28 to 76.\n- [[concepts/on-demand-backup-skill-and-ssd-completeness]] — The job-count completeness tell, which this session extends and complicates by moving the baseline.\n- [[concepts/crashbox-ssd-backup-baseline]] — The SSD leg that now receives working-tree captures for 47 additional repos.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The same day's finding that `DexaProject` is also a live public GitHub repo holding a patient CSV.\n- [[concepts/stack-doctor-sweep-2026-08-11]] — The precedent for resolving repo divergence without adjudicating content on the operator's behalf.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: a clean run over a work set that was never complete.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — Backup repair session (19:17): initial run `status: ERROR` with `~/.claude` one commit behind and 49 unmanifested `~/DEV` folders; user confirmed all 49 mattered (\"we did a migration earlier\"); 47 added as git repos and 2 as plain folders (`_ARCHIVE`, `docs`); job count 28 → 76 and manifest 78 → 125 entries; `AI_Analyst` and `token-dashboard` removed after confirming both had moved to `~/DEV/_ARCHIVE/`; `DexaProject` warning kept as an intentional Google Drive exception; `~/.claude` fixed with `git pull --rebase --autostash` preserving two uncommitted edits; commits `dc54c97` and `38e462b` left unpushed for the next backup run.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Windows /dev-sync Encoding Mangling Manufactured ~200 Phantom Conflict Files\"\naliases: [.from-mac .from-windows files, ΓÇö mojibake, BOM prepended by PowerShell save, portfolio.json 9 updates matched, phantom sync conflicts, dev-sync root cause fix, 95 BOM files 36 repos]\ntags: [git, sync, windows, macos, encoding, data-integrity, powershell, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# Windows /dev-sync Encoding Mangling Manufactured ~200 Phantom Conflict Files\n\nRoughly 200 `.from-mac` / `.from-windows` conflict files had accumulated under `~/DEV` from the Windows↔Mac sync workflow. Root-cause analysis on 2026-09-11 established that none of them were real disagreements: the Windows side of `/dev-sync` was saving files with a command that mangled encoding — em-dash rendered as `ΓÇö` — and prepended a byte-order mark, so git saw every touched file as changed. The fix was applied to the Windows script rather than to the conflict files it produced.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The \"conflicts\" were an artifact of the writer, not of two editors.** A save path that re-encodes text and prepends a BOM changes every byte-sensitive comparison git makes, so files that nobody edited on either side present as divergent.\n- **The claim was proved on a single file rather than asserted.** `portfolio.json` was un-mangled and compared: all 9 apparently-missing updates matched exactly. Nothing had been lost at any point.\n- **The fix went to the root cause.** The Windows `/dev-sync` save command was corrected, so the generator stops producing conflict files, instead of only sweeping up the ~200 already on disk.\n- **95 files across 36 repos still carry BOMs and were deliberately left alone** — some PowerShell scripts genuinely require a BOM to be interpreted correctly, making a blanket strip a separate and riskier job.\n- **17 leftover `.from-*` files from the 9/9 merge were also left in place**: already committed, carrying no risk, and the 9 inside `graphify-out/` are regenerable output that can simply be deleted.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the second consecutive recorded instance of a cross-machine conflict list that collapses almost entirely on inspection. Two days earlier, 36 flagged files reduced to 4 once 26 were identified as CRLF-only differences and 6 as newer generated artifacts. The mechanism here is adjacent but distinct: CRLF normalization makes the working tree lie toward difference as a side effect of a checkout policy, whereas encoding mangling actively rewrites file content on save. The practical consequence is the same and worth stating as a rule — a conflict *count* emitted by any sync tool measures byte divergence, which is a much weaker property than semantic divergence, and the first move on receiving such a list is to classify by kind of difference before resolving a single entry.\n\nThe discipline that distinguishes this session is that the diagnosis was verified against ground truth before the fix was applied. The hypothesis — that the Windows save path was the transforming layer — predicts that reversing the transformation should make the two copies identical. Testing that on `portfolio.json` and finding all 9 updates matching converts a plausible story into a demonstrated one, and simultaneously answers the question that actually mattered to the user, which was not \"why are these files here\" but \"did I lose anything.\" This is the same discriminating-check-before-fixing move applied when `skill-tax` was miscounting skills: two candidate explanations pointed at opposite remedies, so the check had to precede either.\n\nThe deliberate non-actions are as informative as the fix. Stripping 95 BOMs across 36 repos would touch PowerShell scripts where the BOM is load-bearing, converting a cosmetic annoyance into a functional breakage; deferring that work and recording why is the correct call. Likewise, the 17 committed `.from-*` files pose no risk, and the nine under `graphify-out/` are generated output whose Windows-side copies have no independent value. Where this diverges sharply from the 2026-09-09 forecast-log finding is that none of these files are append-only records — there is no accumulating history that a blanket deletion would silently discard, which is precisely the check that made deletion safe here and dangerous there.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The 2026-09-09 predecessor: 36 conflicts collapsing to 4, and the append-only-log exception that makes blanket resolution rules dangerous.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — CRLF normalization as the sibling mechanism by which a working tree reports false differences.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — Windows-side encoding behaviour continuing to shape a repository long after the work moved to a Mac.\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]] — Identifying the transforming layer first, then checking on the far side of it; `portfolio.json` un-mangled is exactly that check.\n- [[connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies]] — A conflict flag is a surface signal; the byte-level comparison beneath it is the ground truth.\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] — A `.from-*` filename asserts a conflict that the content does not support.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-11.md]] — Memory maintenance session (13:10): ~200 `.from-*` files under `~/DEV` traced to a Windows `/dev-sync` save command that mangled encoding (em-dash → `ΓÇö`) and prepended a BOM; proved on `portfolio.json` where all 9 \"missing\" updates matched exactly after un-mangling; Windows-side root cause fixed rather than symptoms swept; 95 BOM-carrying files across 36 repos left alone because some PowerShell scripts need them. Sync session (21:41): 17 remaining `.from-*` files from the 9/9 merge left in place as already-committed and risk-free, with the 9 in `graphify-out` identified as deletable generated output.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/daily-onramp-automation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Daily On-Ramp Automation (Lean Mode)\"\naliases: [on-ramp pilot, onramp.sh, morning briefing automation, must-dos, ctx savings, 4.0 MB savings, 214x compression]\ntags: [automation, productivity, workflow, okrs]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-06-10.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-06-05.md\"\ncreated: 2026-06-10\nupdated: 2026-06-16\n---\n\n# Daily On-Ramp Automation (Lean Mode)\n\nThe Daily On-Ramp is an automated morning-briefing pipeline that gathers raw inputs via a script (`onramp.sh`), distills them into a short \"must-dos\" list, prepends that summary to the day's `morning-briefings/YYYY-MM-DD.md` file, logs an audit row to `onramp-audit.md`, and backs up the result via commit/push. On 2026-06-10 the pipeline was run as a \"lean mode\" pilot; a second documented run occurred on 2026-06-05 using a stripped-down input set (carry-forward items and OKR scoreboard only, with no calendar events or inbox triage yet present for the day).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **\"Lean mode\"** deliberately skips reading raw calendar/email JSON and relies solely on `onramp.sh`'s compact output, trading completeness for token efficiency.\n- **The 2026-06-10 pilot** prepended three sections to that day's briefing: ① Carry-forward items (none — no unchecked items from the prior day), ② 6 must-dos, ③ all 7 active OKR next-actions framed as the week's \"big rocks\"; existing inbox-triage content was preserved below the new section.\n- **The 2026-06-05 run** used a sparse input set — only carry-forward items and the OKR scoreboard, with no calendar events or inbox triage yet available. Produced 5 must-dos prioritizing overdue carry-forward items before OKR big rocks. Demonstrates graceful degradation when inputs are incomplete.\n- **Token efficiency quantified:** the 2026-06-10 lean run measured **536 tokens kept in-context** versus an estimated 114.5K if raw calendar/email data had been read directly — 100% of that volume avoided, logged in `onramp-audit.md`. By 2026-06-05, cumulative savings were **4.0 MB across 22 days / 105 conversations** (a 214× compression ratio before `/compact`), confirming compounding value over multi-week usage.\n- **Audit trail:** each run appends a row to `onramp-audit.md` (format: date, time, yes/no). The on-ramp produces a valid output even on days with minimal inputs; carry-forward items from prior must-do lists seed it when no new sources are available.\n\n## Details\n\nThe pipeline's value proposition is token efficiency: rather than having the assistant read raw calendar and email exports each morning, a lightweight gather script pre-aggregates the relevant signals into a compact form, and the LLM's job is reduced to distillation and formatting. The 2026-06-10 run quantified this trade-off explicitly (536 tokens vs. ~114.5K), giving a concrete per-run baseline for evaluating whether lean mode is \"good enough\" going forward or whether some days will need a fuller run that reads raw sources. By 2026-06-05, the cumulative record across 22 active days and 105 conversations showed 4.0 MB of raw context kept out of the window — at 214× the compressed size — confirming that the \"gather-then-distill\" architecture pays compounding dividends as usage continues. The pipeline's per-run savings are substantial on their own; their cumulative effect across weeks is an order-of-magnitude improvement in context budget efficiency.\n\nThe 2026-06-05 run demonstrated the pipeline's graceful degradation when inputs are sparse: with no calendar events or inbox triage yet available for the day, the on-ramp ran using only carry-forward items and the OKR scoreboard. This produced a valid 5-item must-do list (Ruth's Perch camera upgrade decision overdue carry-forward, uploading practice-planning docs to unblock FaceComps for OKR 1.2, resuming the FaceComps grill and setting rep bands, specifying the unified coach view and volleyball intro/buy-in for OKR 1.1, and advancing basketball summer initiatives) without requiring the full input set. The sparse-input graceful degradation is a key design property: the on-ramp produces useful output regardless of whether all expected sources are present, rather than failing or outputting nothing on low-input days. The must-dos generated by the 2026-06-10 run were derived from inbox/communications triage done earlier that day (16 needs-reply emails, overdue timecards, stale follow-ups, open scheduling items), making the on-ramp a downstream consumer of that triage output rather than an independent process. The same design principle — pre-process inputs so the LLM only reads a compact summary — was applied two months earlier, on 2026-04-14, when the `coleam00/claude-memory-compiler` hook system was set up to summarize sessions into daily logs automatically.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-environment-maintenance]] — The on-ramp pipeline runs inside the same Claude Code harness that was undergoing maintenance earlier on 2026-06-10; the plugin and skill registrations that maintenance pass verified are dependencies for the on-ramp's execution environment.\n- [[concepts/custom-claude-code-skills]] — Both the on-ramp pilot and the new skills built on 2026-06-10 reflect a broader push toward scripted, token-efficient automation layered on top of Claude Code.\n- [[concepts/claude-memory-compiler-setup]] — An earlier (2026-04-14) automation pipeline sharing the same design principle: pre-process inputs so the LLM only reads a compact summary, not raw data. The memory compiler produces the daily logs that the on-ramp's OKR scoreboard and carry-forward items draw from.\n- [[concepts/morning-email-briefing-design]] — A related daily-orientation system (designed 2026-05-21) that addresses email and calendar triage in a structured 6-section format; the on-ramp consumes triage output while the briefing system generates it, making the two pipelines complementary at the input/output boundary.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-06-10.md]] — Pilot run details, must-dos list, ctx stats (536 tokens in-context vs. 114.5K raw), audit/backup confirmation, commit \"on-ramp pilot 2026-06-10.\"\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] — Source of the earlier memory-compiler automation sharing the same gather-then-distill design principle.\n- [[daily/2026-06-05.md]] — Second documented run: carry-forward + OKR scoreboard input set only, 5 must-dos generated, cumulative ctx savings quantified (4.0 MB / 22 days / 105 conversations, 214× compression before /compact).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync False Alarms: Parent-Tracked Folders and Name-Mismatched Repos\"\naliases: [not a repo false alarm, verify before gh repo create, parent repo tracks subfolder, five missing repos name mismatch, Linda Projects path with spaces, parallel fetch loop breaks on spaces, self-cleared repo-diverged]\ntags: [git, sync, workflow, verification, macos, gotcha, github]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-11.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-11\nupdated: 2026-09-12\n---\n\n# DEV Sync False Alarms: Parent-Tracked Folders and Name-Mismatched Repos\n\nA 2026-09-11 `/dev-sync` run reconciling `~/DEV` against the `erash11` GitHub account produced three categories of finding that all evaporated on inspection: four folders flagged as \"not a repo\" were tracked by a parent repository, five repos that appeared missing locally were name mismatches or config repos, and two `repo-diverged` session-start errors had already self-cleared. Nothing was pulled, and the correct conclusion was that nothing was behind.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **\"Not a repo\" from a directory walk usually means \"tracked by a parent repo.\"** A subdirectory of a git repository has no `.git` of its own and will fail a per-folder repo test while being fully version-controlled.\n- **Verify before running `gh repo create`.** Acting on the flag would have created four redundant GitHub repositories and introduced nested-repo hazards where none existed.\n- **Apparently-missing remote repos were name mismatches or config repos**, not absent work — five in total, none requiring action.\n- **The `repo-diverged:global-memory` / `repo-diverged:dotclaude` errors had self-cleared** by the time they were investigated: both repos showed 0 ahead / 0 behind. Session-start errors are a snapshot of a moment that may already have passed.\n- **\"Nothing was behind\" was confirmed by reasoning, not assumed:** Windows last synced 9/9 and all work since has been on the Mac, so an empty pull is the consistent outcome rather than a suspicious one.\n\n## Details\n\nThe unifying property across all three false alarms is that a cheap automated check produced an accurate answer to a question slightly narrower than the one the operator was actually asking. \"Does this directory contain a `.git`?\" is not \"is this directory's content under version control,\" and the gap between them is exactly a subdirectory of a tracked parent. \"Does a remote named `X` exist?\" is not \"is this local work pushed anywhere,\" and the gap is a renamed repository. The operational rule that falls out is to treat the output of a sync scan as a *work queue* rather than a *finding list* — each entry earns a cheap verification before any action, particularly when the proposed action is creative (`gh repo create`) rather than corrective. This mirrors the 2026-09-09 conflict triage, where 36 flagged files reduced to 4, and the same day's repo-visibility audit, where liveness had to be checked by reading inside each repo rather than by trusting a status field.\n\nThe self-clearing divergence errors are a distinct and underappreciated case. Session-start hook errors are generated at a single instant and then persist as text in the session's context for as long as the session runs; by the time an operator reads and investigates one, the underlying condition may have been resolved by a scheduled job, another session, or a subsequent push. Checking the live state first — `0 ahead / 0 behind` — costs seconds and prevents an investigation into a problem that no longer exists. The related instinct, recorded on 2026-09-02 as \"another session may have fixed it,\" applies here in its resolved form: the check was run rather than the assumption made in either direction.\n\nTwo mechanical gotchas round out the session. The parallel fetch loop in the sync script broke on `Linda Projects`, a path containing a space, and had to be handled as a one-off — a standing hazard for any shell loop that word-splits paths and a reason to quote or null-delimit in scripts that walk user directories. Separately, a safety guard blocked a compound Bash command, and splitting it into separate calls succeeded. This is the same guard behaviour that also blocked inline PowerShell via Bash earlier in the day, where the workaround was to write the script to a file and execute it; both are instances of the guard reading a command's *shape* rather than its effect, with the reliable route being to decompose into the simplest form the guard recognises.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] — The same day's sync work, where the root cause of the conflict files was fixed on the Windows side.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The 2026-09-09 precedent of a flagged list collapsing under per-item inspection.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The `~/DEV` → `erash11/DEV` consolidation that established the current repo topology these checks walk.\n- [[concepts/public-repo-privacy-audit-read-inside]] — The companion discipline of verifying repository state by reading inside rather than trusting a listed status.\n- [[concepts/dcg-guard-false-positives-native-tool-workaround]] — The safety guard that blocked the compound Bash command and the inline PowerShell.\n- [[concepts/handoff-doc-uncommitted-tooling-changes]] — The \"another session may have fixed it\" check, applied here to self-cleared divergence errors.\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]] — Repo names and status flags over- and under-including relative to the underlying work.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-11.md]] — Sync session (21:41): `/dev-sync` on the Mac against `erash11`; `repo-diverged:global-memory` and `repo-diverged:dotclaude` found already self-cleared at 0 ahead / 0 behind; four folders flagged \"not a repo\" identified as tracked by a parent repo, with the lesson to verify before running `gh repo create`; five apparently-missing repos identified as name mismatches or config repos; nothing pulled because nothing was behind (Windows last synced 9/9, Mac-only work since); `Linda Projects` breaking the parallel fetch loop on its embedded space; a safety guard blocking a compound Bash command, resolved by splitting into separate calls.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Cross-Machine Conflict File Triage: 36 Files Collapse to 4\"\naliases: [Windows Mac conflict files, 26 CRLF-only conflicts, graphify-out portfolio.json Mac newer, weekly_forecast_log.csv 324 rows, mac wins dangerous for append-only logs, newer is not more complete, SoftwareTech DRAFT 1.0 MB vs 0.4 MB]\ntags: [git, sync, windows, macos, data-integrity, verification, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# Cross-Machine Conflict File Triage: 36 Files Collapse to 4\n\nThirty-six files flagged as conflicting between a Windows machine and a Mac were diffed individually on 2026-09-09 and reduced to four genuine conflicts. Twenty-six differed only by CRLF line endings and six were `graphify-out/` or `portfolio.json` artifacts where the Mac copy is simply newer. The residue produced the session's most consequential finding: a blanket \"Mac wins\" resolution rule silently discards data when the file is an append-only log.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **26 of 36 conflicts were CRLF-only** — a Windows line-ending artifact rather than a content difference, and safe to delete without inspection once identified.\n- **6 more were `graphify-out/` and `portfolio.json` generated files** where the Mac copy is newer and the Windows copy has no independent value.\n- **The 32 safe files can be deleted with zero risk;** only four require human comparison.\n- **\"Mac wins\" is wrong for append-only logs.** `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` is *smaller* on the Mac; the Windows copy holds 324 forecast rows dating back to 2026-08-26 that exist nowhere else. Newer does not mean more complete.\n- **Three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` Word/Excalidraw files remain open**, with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows against 0.4 MB on the Mac — a size gap large enough that neither timestamp nor size alone should decide it.\n\n## Details\n\nThe 89% collapse rate is itself the first lesson: a conflict *count* produced by a sync tool measures byte-level divergence, which is a far weaker property than semantic divergence. CRLF normalization has repeatedly made this stack's working trees lie toward difference, and the same mechanism inflates any cross-machine conflict list generated on a repository that has passed through Windows. Acting on the raw count of 36 would have meant 36 manual comparisons; diffing first cut the human work by an order of magnitude and, more importantly, concentrated attention on the four files where judgment was actually required. The generalizable move is to classify the conflict list by *kind of difference* before resolving any individual entry.\n\nThe forecast-log finding is the durable one and it inverts an intuition that most sync tooling encodes as a default. Last-writer-wins is a reasonable rule for a document, where the newer version is a revision of the older and contains it. It is actively destructive for an append-only log, where the two copies are not revisions of one another but divergent branches of an accumulating record — each machine appended its own rows, and the correct resolution is a *merge*, not a selection. That the Mac copy was smaller is the tell that should trigger the check, and it is a tell that a timestamp-based rule cannot see by construction. The 324 Windows-only rows back to 2026-08-26 are exactly the kind of data whose loss would be discovered months later, during an evaluation, as an unexplained gap — the same class of silent, delayed damage as the `.errors.log` archive overwrite from earlier the same day, and recoverable there only because git happened to hold a second copy.\n\nThe three unresolved `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework` deliverables illustrate the boundary of automated triage. Word and Excalidraw files are opaque binaries whose size difference could mean embedded images, revision history, or genuinely more content, and no diff at the byte level distinguishes those. Deferring them to a hand comparison — rather than applying either machine-wins rule — is the correct call, and the same restraint applied when the two-writer SecondBrain briefing conflict was resolved by staging both sides rather than adjudicating on the spot.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/claude-code-worktree-gotchas]] — CRLF normalization as the mechanism inflating the conflict count.\n- [[connections/windows-defaults-outlive-the-windows-machine]] — CRLF, backslash paths, and `core.symlinks=false` persisting past the Windows machine; this is a further instance.\n- [[concepts/error-log-archive-collision-overwrite]] — The same-day sibling: a careless resolution destroying append-only history, recovered from git by luck.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — The consolidation effort that surfaced the conflict list.\n- [[concepts/cfb-victory-model-evaluation]] — The project whose forecast log holds the 324 at-risk rows; the pregame freeze captures are the file's entire value.\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]] — Identifying the transforming layer (CRLF) before deciding what the difference means.\n- [[connections/step-down-to-ground-truth-when-the-surface-lies]] — A conflict flag is a surface signal; the diff is the ground truth beneath it.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — GitHub consolidation session (22:06): 36 flagged Windows/Mac conflict files diffed down to 4 real ones — 26 CRLF-only, 6 `graphify-out/`/`portfolio.json` where Mac is newer — with the 32 safe files queued for deletion; `CFB_Victory_Model/data/weekly_forecast_log.csv` identified as an append-only log where the Mac-wins rule kept the smaller file and 324 Windows-only rows back to 2026-08-26 exist nowhere else; merge offered but not yet performed; three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework/deliverables/` Word/Excalidraw files left for hand comparison with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows vs 0.4 MB on Mac.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"~/DEV as a Thin Repo: block-all .gitignore and the erash11/DEV Router\"\naliases: [erash11/DEV, thin repo, DEV router files, /* gitignore block-all, 12 tracked files, WINDOWS-SYNC.md, docs/STATUS.md, CLAUDE.md kept as workspace map, GitHub consolidation]\ntags: [git, github, keystoneos, workspace, documentation, macos, windows, multi-model]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-09.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-09\nupdated: 2026-09-09\n---\n\n# ~/DEV as a Thin Repo: block-all .gitignore and the erash11/DEV Router\n\nOn 2026-09-09 the `~/DEV` workspace root was converted into a deliberately thin git repository, `erash11/DEV`, tracking exactly 12 files: the eight router `.md` files plus `.gitignore`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/STATUS.md`, and `docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md`. No project folders are tracked at all. The thinness is enforced by a `.gitignore` whose first line is `/*` — block everything, then explicitly allow the `.md` files back — and that line is recorded as load-bearing.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The `/*` block-all-then-allow pattern is the mechanism, not a stylistic choice.** Removing that first line silently re-admits every project folder under `~/DEV` into the repo; it is annotated so a future cleanup pass does not \"tidy\" it away.\n- **12 tracked files, zero project folders.** The repo exists to version the routing and status layer of the workspace, not its contents — each project keeps its own repository.\n- **`CLAUDE.md` was deliberately *not* collapsed into an `@AGENTS.md` pointer.** It remains a workspace map, and the reason is written inside `AGENTS.md` so the deviation from the usual import pattern is self-documenting.\n- **`docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md` carries queued cross-machine work** in ten numbered sections, handed to the Windows machine as a paste-ready prompt including an explicit instruction not to skip section 5 and to report back on section 10.\n- **The Windows box is active, not retired.** Memory was corrected: an earlier note recording it as retired on 2026-08-14 was wrong, and the personal site is pushed, with `DexaProject` relocated to Google Drive.\n\n## Details\n\nThe thin-repo shape resolves a tension that the multi-model context-file work has been circling for months. `~/DEV` is a workspace root containing dozens of independent repositories, so it cannot itself be a normal repository — nesting would either swallow the children or produce the untracked-nested-repo hazard seen in the skills-repo worktree cleanup. But the routing layer at the root (`AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, the per-domain `.md` routers, a status doc) is exactly the kind of content that benefits from version history, cross-machine sync, and a remote. The block-all `.gitignore` is what lets both be true: the directory is a repo, but its tracked surface is a hand-picked dozen files, and everything else is invisible to git by default rather than by enumeration. This is the inverse of the usual `.gitignore`, which denies by enumeration and therefore drifts as new folders appear — the same drift that produced the 49-folder manifest gap earlier the same day. Default-deny here is structurally correct for the same reason it is correct in the backup manifest described in [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]].\n\nThe `CLAUDE.md` decision is a documented exception to the import-approach convention catalogued in [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]], where a `CLAUDE.md` that has become a parallel copy of `AGENTS.md` is normally collapsed to a one-line `@AGENTS.md` stub. Here the two files serve genuinely different purposes — `AGENTS.md` routes, `CLAUDE.md` maps the workspace — so collapsing would destroy content rather than deduplicate it. Recording the rationale *inside* `AGENTS.md` rather than in a commit message or a session note is the operative move: the file most likely to be read by whoever next considers \"fixing\" the inconsistency is the one carrying the explanation. This is the same discipline that keeps the `DexaProject` backup-manifest warning from being repeatedly re-investigated.\n\n`docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md` makes the repo's second function explicit. Because `~/DEV` now has a remote, it can carry a work queue between two machines that do not share a filesystem, and the ten-section structure plus a paste-ready prompt turns the handoff into something the receiving machine's agent can execute without re-derivation. The two named instructions — do not skip section 5, report back on section 10 — are a small acknowledgement that a long handoff document is likely to be partially executed unless specific sections are called out.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/model-agnostic-repo-dual-context-files]] — The `CLAUDE.md`/`AGENTS.md` convention this repo deliberately departs from, with the reason recorded in-file.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The same default-deny logic applied to backup scope rather than git scope.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The same-day session whose 49-folder gap illustrates what enumeration-based inclusion costs.\n- [[concepts/keystoneos-project-registry]] — The registry layer this router surface indexes.\n- [[concepts/handoff-document-placement-discipline]] — Handoff docs belong in the project directory; `WINDOWS-SYNC.md` follows that rule and gains a remote as a bonus.\n- [[connections/git-repository-boundaries-should-be-explicit]] — A workspace root that is a repo needs its boundary declared, not inferred.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-09.md]] — GitHub consolidation session (22:06): `~/DEV` converted to the thin repo `erash11/DEV` tracking 12 files (8 router `.md` files plus `.gitignore`, `AGENTS.md`, `docs/STATUS.md`, `docs/WINDOWS-SYNC.md`) with zero project folders; `.gitignore` starting with `/*` recorded as the load-bearing line; `CLAUDE.md` kept as a workspace map with the rationale written into `AGENTS.md`; memory corrected to mark the Windows box active rather than retired 8/14; ten-section `WINDOWS-SYNC.md` queue handed off with explicit section-5 and section-10 instructions.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/git-fetch-before-declaring-clean.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"\\\"Clean\\\" Is Meaningless Without git fetch\"\naliases: [140 clean repos wrong, un-fetched sync pass, 5 repos behind after fetch, dev-sync fetch first, local-state-only clean claim, advisor caught missing fetch]\ntags: [git, sync, verification, workflow, macos, gotcha, dev-sync]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-19.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-19\nupdated: 2026-09-29\n---\n\n# \"Clean\" Is Meaningless Without git fetch\n\nA 2026-09-19 `/dev-sync` run on the Mac reported roughly 140 repositories clean on its first pass. An advisor check noticed that the pass had never run `git fetch`, so every \"clean\" verdict described only local working-tree state and said nothing about the remote. Re-running with a fetch across all repositories revealed five repos behind (`dotclaude` +7, `claude-skills` +1, `global-memory` +8, `DEV` root +2, `Gemini_Projects` +2, plus the SecondBrain vault +2). The session recorded this as the single biggest failure mode of the sync skill.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`git status` without a prior `git fetch` reports against a stale remote-tracking ref**, so \"up to date with origin/main\" is a claim about the last time the remote was contacted, not about the remote.\n- **The failure is silent and confidence-inspiring.** 140 clean repos is exactly the output a healthy sync should produce, so the wrong answer is indistinguishable from the right one without knowing whether fetch ran.\n- **The fetched re-run found five repos behind**, all of them the shared config and memory repos that the other machine writes to most — precisely the ones where staleness costs the most.\n- **The catch came from a review layer, not the run itself**, since nothing in the sync output records whether a fetch preceded the status checks.\n- **The remedy is structural:** fetch unconditionally before reporting sync status, rather than treating fetch as an optimization to skip.\n\n## Details\n\nThis is the same shape as several findings already in the record: a check answers a narrower predicate than the operational question being asked of it. `git status` faithfully answers \"does my working tree differ from what I last knew of the remote?\" — a question nobody asked. The operational question is \"does my working tree differ from the remote?\", and the gap between them is exactly one network round-trip. Because the narrow answer is phrased in the vocabulary of the broad one (\"up to date with 'origin/main'\"), there is no textual tell that the answer is scoped; the same trap catalogued in [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] for `launchctl`'s scheduled-run-only exit code and in [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] for a status field that reports on a shrunken work set.\n\nWhat makes this instance expensive is the direction of the error. An un-fetched pass is biased toward reporting *health*: every repo the remote has advanced past still looks current, so the tool's failure mode is to under-report work rather than over-report it. This is the inverse of [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]], where the same skill's flags substantially *overstated* the problem and each finding evaporated on inspection. Taken together the two runs bracket the skill's reliability: its positive findings need verification before action, and its negative findings — the clean verdicts — need verification that the check was even capable of finding something. A tool that can be wrong in both directions is a work queue, not a report.\n\nThe practical fix is to make fetch non-optional in the sync path rather than to remember to run it. Fetch is cheap, read-only, and cannot damage a working tree, so there is no cost model under which skipping it is correct; the only reason it was skipped is that nothing forced it. Compare the discipline in [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]], where a state change is permitted only after a verified re-run — here the reporting step should be gated on the fetch having happened, so that a sync report either includes fetch evidence or declines to claim cleanliness. The run that followed the fix was genuinely clean: 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The same skill erring in the opposite direction, overstating problems that evaporated on inspection.\n- [[connections/status-field-scope-narrower-than-the-question]] — The general trap: a field reporting truthfully on a narrower predicate than the question asked.\n- [[concepts/backup-status-ok-versus-job-count]] — A status field reporting OK over a work set that had silently shrunk.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — A neighbouring git indicator read as a broader operational fact than it supports.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — The next sync run, which walked 146 repos with the fetch discipline in place.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — The precedent that a verified re-run, not an intended fix, licenses the state change.\n- [[connections/every-layer-reported-healthy-except-the-destination]] — Locally truthful stage reports that do not compose into end-to-end assurance.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-19.md]] — Morning `/dev-sync` session (09:17): first inventory pass reported repos clean without running `git fetch`, advisor flagged that the claim rested only on local state, re-run with fetch across all repos revealed 5 behind; recorded as \"the single biggest failure mode of this skill,\" with the final clean result of 143 repos walked, 0 conflicts, 0 `.from-*` files, and incoming commits to `dotclaude` (+7), `claude-skills` (+1), `global-memory` (+8), `DEV` root (+2), `Gemini_Projects` (+2), and the SecondBrain vault (+2).\n\n```\n\n### concepts/preregistration-as-substitute-for-external-review.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Pre-Registration as a Substitute for an Absent External Reviewer\"\naliases: [pre-registration timestamp, who is the reviewer question, self-review safeguard, commit before the data exists, timestamp-verifiable thresholds, negative result still published, ADR only for real trade-offs]\ntags: [methodology, statistics, process, adr, review, epistemics, git]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-08-08.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-01\nupdated: 2026-09-01\n---\n\n# Pre-Registration as a Substitute for an Absent External Reviewer\n\nWhen an analysis has no second reader, the usual defense against post-hoc threshold tuning — someone else checking the reasoning — is unavailable. The S2_Evaluation session on 2026-08-08 surfaced this explicitly (Q25: \"who is the sports science reviewer?\" — the user confirmed it was himself) and answered it structurally: commit the thresholds and decision records to version control **before the data exists**, so the pre-registration carries a verifiable timestamp instead of an assertion.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Asking \"who reviews this?\" is a design question, not an administrative one.** The answer changes which safeguards are load-bearing; a design that assumes a reviewer and does not have one is unguarded in a way nobody notices.\n- **A timestamp in git is falsifiable; a claim in a document is not.** Nine ADRs were committed at 22:46:32, before the CSV export existed, making \"these thresholds were fixed in advance\" checkable by a third party rather than taken on trust.\n- **Pre-commitment must include the negative outcome.** S2 pre-declared that a negative gate result — no athlete clears the 1.5-SD threshold — is still written up and published. Without that rule, a threshold silently becomes a filter for interesting-looking output.\n- **Absolute thresholds pre-commit; relative ones do not.** \"≥1.5 population SD\" can return zero names; \"top 3 by deviation\" always returns three, so it encodes no prior claim about what counts as unusual and cannot be violated by the data.\n- **Record decisions only where trade-offs are real.** ADRs were written for consequential choices and deliberately omitted for cheap-to-reverse presentation questions, keeping the decision log a signal rather than a transcript.\n\n## Details\n\nThe mechanism this defends against is not dishonesty but ordinary drift. An analyst who sets a threshold after seeing the data cannot fully separate \"this cut point is principled\" from \"this cut point produces a plausible-looking list of names,\" and no amount of good faith resolves the ambiguity from the inside. External review normally breaks the loop by introducing a reader who did not see the intermediate results. When that reader does not exist, the loop must be broken temporally instead: the decision is fixed at a point where the data could not have influenced it, and that point is made externally verifiable. Git supplies exactly this — an authenticated ordering of commits that a skeptic can inspect without trusting the author's account of when they decided what.\n\nThe distinction between absolute and relative thresholds is what makes the pre-registration bite. A relative rule (\"flag the top 3\") is unfalsifiable by construction: whatever the data look like, it produces the same quantity of output, so committing to it in advance commits to nothing. An absolute rule (\"flag anyone past 1.5 population SD\") encodes a substantive prior claim about what magnitude is worth a coach's attention, and it can be wrong — it can return nobody. That possibility is the whole point, which is why the companion rule (publish the negative result) is not an afterthought. A threshold that is only reported when it fires has been quietly converted back into a selection procedure. The same logic explains why the room-size floor came with a *renamed* fallback rather than a silent one: changing the comparison population changes what the number means, so the artifact's claim has to change with it.\n\nThe practice generalizes beyond statistics to any output whose quality cannot be checked from the output alone. The recurring theme across this knowledge base — verify at ground truth rather than trusting the layer above — usually applies downstream, to results. Pre-registration is the upstream form: when downstream verification is impossible because there is no independent channel to verify against, move the check earlier, to a point where the reasoning can still be pinned down and time-stamped. The cost is small and front-loaded; the alternative is an analysis whose thresholds are, in principle, unauditable. A related discipline is to keep the record proportional: writing an ADR for every choice dilutes the log until the consequential commitments are indistinguishable from formatting preferences, so only decisions with genuine trade-offs earn a record.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/s2-evaluation-preregistered-analysis-design]] — The analysis this practice was adopted for; contains the specific thresholds, cohort rules, and metric that were pre-registered.\n- [[concepts/tezino-camp-prep-spc-monitoring]] — Its ADR-0001 validation-honesty decision is the same commitment to reporting what the method cannot support, made in advance.\n- [[connections/refuse-upstream-or-verify-downstream]] — Pre-registration is the upstream branch of that dichotomy, chosen precisely because no independent downstream channel exists.\n- [[concepts/forecast-slicing-on-own-output-bias]] — A concrete case of an analysis choice made after seeing output manufacturing an artifact; pre-registration is the structural defense against that class of error.\n- [[concepts/codex-adversarial-verification]] — The alternative answer to the same question: import a second model as the reviewer rather than substituting a temporal safeguard.\n- [[concepts/subagent-driven-development]] — Multi-stage agent review as the in-session form of external checking; where it is unavailable, pre-registration fills the gap.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-08-08.md]] — S2_Evaluation close-out (backfilled 2026-08-29 14:55): Q25 reframing the project on confirming the user is his own reviewer, nine ADRs committed at 22:46:32 before the CSV existed, absolute ≥1.5 SD threshold chosen over \"top 3 by deviation\", negative gate result pre-committed to publication, ADRs written only for decisions with real trade-offs.\n\n```\n\n### concepts/skylar-zinnecker-cmj-rtc-reports.md\n```markdown\n---\ntitle: \"Skylar Zinnecker CMJ Return-to-Competition Reports\"\naliases: [Skylar Zinnecker, CMJ RTC reports, Post-Op Low, VALD ForceDecks export, Baylor athlete report]\ntags: [sports-science, vald, cmj, force-plate, reporting, baylor-athletics]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-04-14.md\"\ncreated: 2026-04-14\nupdated: 2026-04-14\n---\n\n# Skylar Zinnecker CMJ Return-to-Competition Reports\n\nA series of CMJ (countermovement jump) force-plate performance reports were built for Baylor Athletics athlete Skylar Zinnecker on 2026-04-14, to support a return-to-competition (RTC) decision following injury and surgery. The reports were delivered as polished, print-ready HTML files using Chart.js, and were followed by a terminology cleanup and project documentation pass.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Source data was a VALD ForceDecks export (`CMJ-test-export-04_14_2026.csv`, 67 sessions spanning August 2023-April 2026), processed into HTML reports with Chart.js visualizations.\n- Injury (April 12, 2025) and surgery (April 25, 2025) dates were annotated as vertical markers on the charts.\n- The baseline was redefined from a full 2-year historical average to the last 4 pre-injury sessions (March 20-April 10, 2025) - a clinically more appropriate baseline for an RTC assessment - while \"current\" values used a 3-session rolling average from April 2, 7, and 8, 2026.\n- The baseline change flipped the Lower-Limb Stiffness metric's status from \"Progressing\" (⚠) to \"Exceeds Baseline\" (✓), since the new baseline (4,693) was lower than the old one (5,477); asymmetry metrics were normalized inversely (lower % = better) for the radar chart.\n- The term \"nadir\"/\"Injury Nadir\" (the post-surgery performance floor used in the recovery-percentage formula) was replaced with the clinically clearer \"Post-Op Low\" across all three HTML files (9 instances total), after the user found \"nadir\" too jargon-heavy for a clinical audience.\n\n## Details\n\nStyling followed Baylor's institutional branding (`#1e4d2b` green, `#b87d0a` gold) in light mode, with full print CSS (`@page` set to letter portrait, plus `-webkit-print-color-adjust: exact` to preserve colors when printed). The athlete portrait (`SZ.jpg`) was referenced via a relative path rather than embedded as base64, meaning the image and HTML files must be kept in the same directory for the reports to render correctly.\n\nSeveral practical lessons emerged during the build. The `Edit` tool fails outright if its `old_string` argument doesn't match the file exactly, so the working pattern became: use `Grep` to confirm the exact existing text, re-read the surrounding context, and only then call `Edit`. A PowerShell image-dimension check required `Add-Type -AssemblyName System.Drawing` to be loaded before `[System.Drawing.Image]` could be used. Duplicate JS object keys (e.g., in a color palette) can silently survive an `Edit`, so any palette change was verified afterward with `Grep`. After all six requested deliverables were completed and saved to `C:\\Users\\eric_rash\\Desktop\\DEV\\SpecialRequests\\Zinnecker_Skylar\\`, a follow-up session ran `/init` to generate `CLAUDE.md` and `session_notes.md`, documenting the project for any future session that picks it back up.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/baylor-load-simulator-deployment]] - Another Baylor Athletics sports-science deliverable completed the same day (2026-04-14).\n- [[concepts/keystone-os-tezino-project]] - A later (2026-06-10) Baylor Athletics project that processes the same kind of VALD ForceDecks CMJ data (including the mRSI metric) for a different purpose; see [[connections/vald-forcedecks-cmj-data-across-projects]].\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-04-14.md]] - Report-building session (17:27) and terminology/documentation follow-up session (17:55).\n\n```\n\nThese are the 13 articles (of 309) most related to this log, plus graph neighbours and least-linked articles. This is the relevant context, not a partial view to be cautious about.\n- The Current Wiki Index above lists EVERY article. Consult it before creating a new one, and you may [[wikilink]] any article listed there even if its text is not shown.\n- If this log adds detail, correction, or a new example to an article shown above, UPDATE it: re-emit it at its existing path with the full revised content. Updating existing articles is a primary expected outcome, not a fallback.\n\n## Daily Log to Compile\n\n**File:** 2026-09-29.md\n\n# Daily Log: 2026-09-29\n\n## Sessions\n\n## Memory Maintenance\n\n### Session (08:46)\n\n**Context:** Ran `/dev-sync` on the Mac to pull GitHub state from the Windows laptop, then picked up the Flint / Drive-triage thread.\n\n**Key Exchanges:**\n- Asked which repo to resume: TypeSafe_Sandbox (newest active) vs Flint (newer commits but not yet on the active list). Eric clarified the Drive triage work belongs to Flint.\n- Flint's books, OCR and audio exist only on Windows, so atomizing and OCR must run there. The Mac can still run the Drive triage test, because `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` (200-file sample) is in git and Drive is mounted on the Mac.\n\n**Decisions Made:**\n- Resume Flint, with the Drive triage (Jev pilot) executed inside TypeSafe_Sandbox.\n- Router conflict on `RESEARCH.md`: Windows had already added Flint, so the Mac's duplicate line was dropped and the Windows line kept. Resolved by hand, no `.from-*` files created.\n- No Jev API call happens until Claude's blind answer key is written and committed.\n\n**Lessons Learned:**\n- Two machines independently added the same router entry for a new project — duplicate-entry conflicts are the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge, not a mangled-file problem.\n- Ranking repos by commit-days (not recency) worked: 26 active vs 115 dormant-and-clean. Flint is genuinely new work but still does not appear on the active list, so the \"newest active repo\" pick alone would have chosen the wrong thread — Eric's clarification was needed.\n\n**Action Items:**\n- Run `smoke_test.py` — the TypeSafe API key on the Mac has never been tested.\n- Build the triage state for the 200 sample files, then write and commit the blind answer key before the first Jev call.\n- Investigate `perch-pull` failures (9/26–9/27): log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` — not yet opened.\n- `SecondBrain` is still behind GitHub; dev-sync does not cover it. Needs a separate pull.\n- `RunningTheRoom` is missing from the daily-backup manifest (`manifest-unmanaged`), recurring since 9/24.\n- Memory compiler is backlogged a few logs; a flush failed 9/27 on a session limit.\n- Open question for Eric: go to maintenance mode on TypeSafe, or keep testing?\n\n\n\n## Your Task\n\nDo NOT use any tools and do NOT attempt to write files — your sandbox cannot\nwrite under this directory. Instead, emit the articles as plain text using the\nexact delimiter format below. The orchestrator parses your output and writes the\nfiles itself.\n\n### Output format\n\nFor each new or updated article, emit a block:\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/<slug>.md===\n<full markdown: complete YAML frontmatter + body, per the AGENTS.md schema>\n===END ARTICLE===\n\nUse `connections/<slug>.md` for connection articles and `qa/<slug>.md` for Q&A.\nSlugs are lowercase and hyphenated. To UPDATE an existing article, emit it with\nits existing path and the full revised content (it overwrites).\n\nAfter the article blocks, emit exactly one index block and one log block:\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/<slug>]] | one-line summary | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-10-01T06:03:55-05:00] compile | 2026-09-29.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-29.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/<slug>]]\n- Articles updated: (none)\n===END LOG===\n\n### Rules\n1. Identify 3-7 distinct concepts worth their own article.\n2. Emit ===NOTHING=== ONLY when the log itself contains no durable knowledge\n   (e.g. \"FLUSH_OK\", or purely transient chatter). \"An article already covers\n   this topic\" is NOT a reason to emit ===NOTHING=== - it is a reason to UPDATE\n   that article with whatever this log adds.\n3. Every article: complete YAML frontmatter (title, sources, created, updated)\n   with `sources` citing 2026-09-29.md; at least 2 [[wikilinks]]; Key Points\n   3-5 bullets; Details 2+ paragraphs; Related Concepts 2+ entries; encyclopedia\n   style — neutral and comprehensive. Follow the AGENTS.md article format exactly.\n4. Create a connection article when the log reveals a non-obvious relationship\n   between 2+ concepts.\n4b. Do NOT re-emit an article whose only change would be the `updated:` date or\n   a restatement of what it already says. Measured on a real run, 4 of 5\n   \"updates\" changed nothing but the timestamp - that is churn: it rewrites\n   files, dirties backups, and invalidates caches while adding no knowledge.\n   Re-emit an article only when you are adding substantive content.\n5. Add one ===INDEX=== table row per article (new or updated).\n\nEmit ONLY the delimiter blocks — no preamble, no commentary.\n"}
