{"parentUuid":"0d4834ac-deaa-43de-a9e3-b46357ada3f5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfbQWj3pNByssXv5tVpHG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89567,"cache_read_input_tokens":9707,"output_tokens":24713,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4950},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89567,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":24713,"cache_read_input_tokens":9707,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89567,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89567},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfbQWigVrgs5E4LFUiJPa","type":"assistant","uuid":"f96fbfe6-1c78-4cd7-87e5-e4fa9f0a6784","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:05:08.933Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cc7a7290-35b1-45f8-8a39-c4c00a47afa6","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f96fbfe6-1c78-4cd7-87e5-e4fa9f0a6784","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfbQWj3pNByssXv5tVpHG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"===ARTICLE concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Duplicate-Entry Conflicts Are the Expected Shape of a Router-Index Merge\"\naliases: [RESEARCH.md conflict, duplicate router line, both machines added Flint, dedupe not union, router index is a set not a log, keep the Windows line drop the Mac line, no .from-* files created]\ntags: [git, sync, merge, workspace, documentation, macos, windows, gotcha]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-29\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Duplicate-Entry Conflicts Are the Expected Shape of a Router-Index Merge\n\nDuring the 2026-09-29 `/dev-sync` run, `~/DEV/RESEARCH.md` came back in conflict: the Windows laptop had already added a router entry for the new `Flint` project, and the Mac had independently added its own line for the same project. The resolution was to keep the Windows line and drop the Mac's duplicate — a deduplication, not a union and not a newest-wins pick. It was done by hand and produced no `.from-*` conflict files. The session recorded the general form: a duplicate entry is what a `RESEARCH.md` conflict normally looks like, and it should not be investigated as a mangled-file problem.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Both sides were correct.** Neither machine made a mistake; each observed the same new project and recorded the same fact, so git's conflict reflects concurrent *agreement* rather than disagreement.\n- **The correct merge is set-union by identity, which in practice means deduplicate.** Keeping both lines — the right answer for an append-only log — would list `Flint` twice in the router.\n- **Newest-wins is also wrong,** because the two lines are not revisions of one another; whichever is kept, nothing is lost and nothing is gained.\n- **Recognising the shape is the time saver.** A prior sync session spent real effort root-causing ~200 conflict files to a Windows save command that mangled encoding; a duplicate router line looks superficially similar in a conflict list but needs no investigation at all.\n- **New-project creation is exactly when both machines touch the router**, so this conflict class is structurally likely rather than incidental.\n\n## Details\n\nThe `~/DEV` thin repo exists to version a small routing-and-status surface — eight router `.md` files plus a handful of docs — while tracking no project folders at all (see [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]]). That design concentrates cross-machine contention onto precisely the files most likely to be edited on both machines at once: a new project is created on one machine, noticed on the other, and each session dutifully adds a router entry. The conflict is therefore a predictable by-product of the repo's purpose rather than a sign that anything is wrong with the sync path, and the right posture on seeing it is to deduplicate and move on.\n\nWhat makes the case instructive is that it is a fourth distinct merge semantics for cross-machine conflicts in this workspace, and the fourth to require a different verb. Transcript logs under `global-memory/transcripts/` are append-only and merge by keeping every line from both sides ([[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]). `weekly_forecast_log.csv` is an accumulating record whose Mac copy was *smaller*, so it had to be merged by key rather than selected ([[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]). The ~200 `.from-*` files traced to a Windows save command were pure transform artifacts with no content difference at all ([[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]). A router index is none of these: it is a set keyed by project identity, so a repeated key is a duplicate to collapse. This also explains why configuring a textual merge driver — the fix adopted for the transcript logs — would be actively harmful here, since a union driver would produce exactly the doubled entry the correct resolution removes.\n\nThe practical rule is to read the artifact's kind before reaching for a resolution, and to accept that for a small, human-readable index the cheapest correct tool is a hand edit. The cost of that edit is a few seconds; the cost of applying an automated strategy derived from a different artifact class is a quietly corrupted router that misdirects a future session. That the resolution left no `.from-*` files behind is the secondary tell that the right strategy was used — the conflict-file machinery exists to defer adjudication to a human, and when there is nothing to adjudicate it should produce nothing to clean up.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — The append-only transcript logs whose correct resolution (keep both) is the opposite of this one.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline, and the accumulating-record case where a smaller newer file was the tell.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]] — Conflicts that were pure save-path artifacts, the case this one should not be mistaken for.\n- [[concepts/dev-thin-repo-github-consolidation]] — Why the router files are the workspace's highest-contention surface.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why a timestamp carries no claim about content when the two copies are not revisions.\n- [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]] — The synthesis this instance completes.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46): router conflict on `RESEARCH.md` where Windows had already added `Flint` and the Mac's duplicate line was dropped, resolved by hand with no `.from-*` files created; recorded as the lesson that duplicate-entry conflicts are the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge rather than a mangled-file problem.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/blind-answer-key-before-first-api-call.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Write and Commit the Blind Answer Key Before the First API Call\"\naliases: [Jev pilot answer key, blind answer key gate, no API call until the key is committed, Drive triage 200-file sample, grade before you see the output, Claude labels the sample first]\ntags: [methodology, evaluation, epistemics, git, llm, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-29\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Write and Commit the Blind Answer Key Before the First API Call\n\nThe Flint / Google Drive triage pilot — classifying Drive files using the Jev service over a committed 200-file sample — was gated on a single precondition recorded on 2026-09-29: no Jev API call happens until Claude's own blind answer key is written and committed. The grader's labels are produced without having seen the system-under-test's output, and the commit makes that ordering verifiable rather than merely asserted.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The gate is stated as a hard precondition, not an intention.** \"No API call until the key is committed\" halts the pipeline at the first unmet condition rather than trusting a later resolution to be honest.\n- **Blind means the labels precede the output, not merely that they were produced independently.** Once the vendor's classifications are visible, a grader cannot separate \"this label is right\" from \"this label agrees with what I just read.\"\n- **Committing the key supplies a timestamp a third party can check**, which is what distinguishes pre-registration from a claim in a document.\n- **The 200-file sample is what makes a blind key affordable at all.** Hand-labelling a full Drive would be infeasible; a bounded, committed sample converts an impossible safeguard into an hour of work.\n- **The evaluator and the operator are the same person here**, which is precisely the condition under which a temporal safeguard has to stand in for an external reviewer.\n\n## Details\n\nThe failure this guards against is anchoring, not dishonesty. A triage classifier's output is plausible by construction — that is what makes it useful and what makes it hard to grade. Reading the vendor's label for a file before deciding what the file actually is collapses two separate judgements into one, and the collapse is invisible from the inside: every individual decision feels principled while the aggregate accuracy figure drifts upward. The same mechanism produced the manufactured bias in [[concepts/forecast-slicing-on-own-output-bias]], where conditioning an evaluation subset on the model's own forecast created an 8.1-point artifact that vanished at scale. Fixing the labels first removes the opportunity rather than relying on discipline to resist it.\n\nStructurally this is the same move as the S2 evaluation's nine ADRs committed before the CSV existed, described in [[concepts/preregistration-as-substitute-for-external-review]]: when there is no second reader, the only remaining safeguard is a verifiable ordering in time, and git supplies one for free. The variation here is that the pre-committed artifact is a *ground-truth set* rather than a *threshold*, which has a useful side effect — a blind key also measures the grader. Disagreements split into cases where the vendor is wrong and cases where the key is wrong, and the second category is only recoverable because the key was fixed in advance and can be re-examined as written rather than as remembered. Review by a second model, as in [[concepts/codex-adversarial-verification]], is the alternative answer to the same question; the two are complementary, since an independent reviewer cannot audit decision *order* and a timestamp cannot supply a fresh frame.\n\nTwo practical corollaries follow. First, the key must be committed, not merely written: an uncommitted file on disk has no verifiable creation time and is indistinguishable from one edited after the fact. Second, the sample has to be the thing committed too — `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` being in git is what lets the key refer to a fixed population rather than a moving one, and is separately what let the test run on a machine that does not hold the corpus (see [[concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing]]). A pre-registered key over an unpinned sample would leave the population free to drift, which reopens the hole from the other side.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/preregistration-as-substitute-for-external-review]] — The parent practice: a git timestamp substituting for an absent reviewer.\n- [[concepts/forecast-slicing-on-own-output-bias]] — A concrete case of an evaluation choice made after seeing output manufacturing a result.\n- [[concepts/s2-evaluation-preregistered-analysis-design]] — The fullest prior application, where thresholds and cohort rules were fixed before the data existed.\n- [[concepts/codex-adversarial-verification]] — The complementary safeguard: a second model as reviewer, which cannot audit decision order.\n- [[concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing]] — The committed sample this key is defined over, and what it bought operationally.\n- [[connections/refuse-at-the-earliest-unmet-precondition]] — The gate's form: halt before the first API call rather than remediate after it.\n- [[connections/reviewers-cannot-audit-decision-order]] — Why layered review is structurally blind to the error a timestamp catches.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46): decision that no Jev API call happens until Claude's blind answer key is written and committed, with the action item to build the triage state for the 200 sample files and then write and commit the key before the first call.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing.md===\n---\ntitle: \"A Committed Sample Decouples Pipeline Work From Corpus Locality\"\naliases: [sample.csv in git, 200-file sample, Flint books only on Windows, OCR must run on Windows, Drive mounted on the Mac, test the leg that has its data, drive_triage inside TypeSafe_Sandbox]\ntags: [workflow, cross-machine, git, data-management, windows, macos, pipeline]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-29\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# A Committed Sample Decouples Pipeline Work From Corpus Locality\n\nResuming the Flint project on 2026-09-29 produced a split verdict on what could be worked on from the Mac. Flint's books, OCR output, and audio exist only on the Windows laptop, so atomizing and OCR are Windows-only work. The Google Drive triage leg, however, was Mac-runnable: `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` — a 200-file sample — is committed to git, and Drive is mounted on the Mac. The large, unportable corpus blocked one leg; a small committed sample freed the other.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **The blocking resource is the corpus, not the code.** Books, OCR output, and audio are the artifacts that cannot travel through git, so every leg that reads them inherits the Windows constraint.\n- **A committed sample converts a machine-bound pipeline into a machine-independent one** for development and testing purposes, because git already replicates it to every machine.\n- **The partition is per-leg, not per-project.** Flint as a whole is \"the Windows project\"; the triage leg within it is not, and asking the question leg-by-leg is what surfaced the available work.\n- **Where the sample lives decides where the work happens.** The triage was executed inside `TypeSafe_Sandbox` — the repo holding the sample — even though the project of record is Flint.\n- **Code portability does not imply credential portability.** The Mac's TypeSafe API key had never been exercised, leaving `smoke_test.py` as an open action item: the leg can run here only once the key is proven here.\n\n## Details\n\nThe useful generalisation is that cross-machine work partitioning should be done against each leg's *inputs and mounts* rather than against the project label. A project is a convenient unit for ownership and a poor unit for locality, because its legs typically depend on different resources: source code and small fixtures travel through git, large media and OCR output do not, a mounted cloud drive is available wherever it is mounted, and credentials are machine-local state of a third kind. Enumerating those per leg is exactly the triage already formalised for network dependence in [[concepts/offline-capable-pipeline-legs]] — local, deferred, or dependent — applied here along a locality axis rather than a connectivity one. The answer it produces is more useful than a yes/no on the project: two of Flint's legs are parked on Windows and one is live on the Mac today.\n\nCommitting a bounded sample is the deliberate intervention that makes this possible, and it pays twice. Operationally it is the only reason any triage work could happen on the Mac at all. Methodologically it pins the evaluation population, which is what lets a blind answer key refer to a fixed set of files rather than a moving target — the gate described in [[concepts/blind-answer-key-before-first-api-call]] would be far weaker over an unpinned corpus. The cost is the usual sampling caveat: 200 files are representative only by assumption, so results established on the sample are provisional until the full run on Windows, and the sample's selection criteria deserve to be recorded alongside it.\n\nThe open `smoke_test.py` item marks the limit of what a committed sample buys. Code and data arriving on a machine do not establish that the machine can make the call — API keys, mounted paths, and installed interpreters are host facts captured per machine, and an untested key is a precondition that has never been exercised. This is the same hazard catalogued in [[connections/install-time-environment-capture-rots-silently]] from the other direction: there, a captured host fact had gone stale; here, it has never been verified at all. Running the smoke test before the pilot is the cheap gate, and it is a precondition of the same shape as the answer-key commit — something to prove before the first paid call rather than diagnose after it.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/blind-answer-key-before-first-api-call]] — The evaluation gate defined over this committed sample.\n- [[concepts/offline-capable-pipeline-legs]] — The per-leg dependency triage this applies along a data-locality axis.\n- [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]] — The complementary pattern: do everything git can carry from the machine you are on, defer only what the other machine's local state requires.\n- [[connections/install-time-environment-capture-rots-silently]] — Credentials and interpreter paths as per-machine facts that code portability does not cover.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — The sync run that delivered the sample and router state to the Mac in the first place.\n- [[concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work]] — The same session's thread-selection problem, which determined that Flint was the project to resume.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46): Flint's books, OCR, and audio existing only on Windows making atomizing and OCR Windows-only, while the Drive triage test was Mac-runnable because `TypeSafe_Sandbox/drive_triage/sample.csv` (200-file sample) is in git and Drive is mounted on the Mac; decision to resume Flint with the Jev-pilot triage executed inside `TypeSafe_Sandbox`; `smoke_test.py` left open because the Mac's TypeSafe API key had never been tested.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Carried-Forward Maintenance Residue: Per-Tool Scope Gaps and Unread Diagnostics\"\naliases: [SecondBrain not covered by dev-sync, manifest-unmanaged RunningTheRoom recurring, flush failed on session limit, perch-pull failures 9/26 9/27, last_run.json not yet opened, one command each but carried for days]\ntags: [maintenance, backup, sync, automation, verification, macos, workflow]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-29\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Carried-Forward Maintenance Residue: Per-Tool Scope Gaps and Unread Diagnostics\n\nThe 2026-09-29 session closed with four maintenance items that were each a single command away from resolution and were each carried forward anyway: `SecondBrain` behind GitHub and outside `/dev-sync`'s scope, `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` recurring since 9/24, the memory compiler backlogged a few logs with a flush having failed on 9/27 against a session limit, and `perch-pull` failures from 9/26–9/27 whose log path was recorded but never opened. Together they illustrate two durable shapes: coverage is declared per tool rather than global, and a diagnostic that has been located is not thereby read.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **`SecondBrain` falls outside `/dev-sync`'s scope entirely**, so a clean sync report — however many repos it walks — makes no claim about the vault. It needs a separate pull.\n- **Coverage gaps run in both directions across tools.** The vault is in the backup manifest but not in sync; `RunningTheRoom` is in the workspace but not in the manifest. There is no single question whose answer is \"everything is covered.\"\n- **The 9/27 flush failure means the capture tier can lose a day, not just the compile tier** — and it failed against a session limit, so the days most worth capturing are the likeliest to be dropped.\n- **`perch-pull`'s log was located but not opened.** `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` was named in the action items with the explicit note \"not yet opened,\" which is an honest record of a diagnosis deferred rather than performed.\n- **Cheapness is why these recur.** Each item is one command, so each is easy to defer; `manifest-unmanaged` has now been deferred across at least three sessions on this basis.\n\n## Details\n\nThe scope-gap half of this is the recurring structural finding of the whole maintenance programme, and this session supplies an unusually clean statement of it: `/dev-sync` walks the repos it knows about and says nothing whatsoever about the Obsidian vault, while the daily backup manifest covers the vault and omits `RunningTheRoom`. Neither tool is broken and neither reports an error about the other's gap, because each is answering truthfully about its own declared set — the shape catalogued in [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] and [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]]. The operational consequence is that \"is my work safe and in sync?\" is not a question any single tool's green status answers, and the union of the tools' scopes has never been checked against the workspace's actual contents. `RunningTheRoom`'s third consecutive appearance pushes this past the threshold where another manual manifest line is the wrong fix; by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]], recurrence is the argument for making the manifest discover rather than declare, as [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] already proposes.\n\nThe compiler backlog is now a two-tier problem rather than one. The known condition is a compile deferred on cost grounds, with the warning deliberately left standing in `.errors.log` until a verified clean run clears it ([[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]], [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]]). What 9/27 adds is a failure one tier *upstream*: a flush — the capture step that writes the daily log in the first place — failed against a session limit. A deferred compile loses only timeliness, because the source log survives and can be compiled later; a failed flush loses the source. That the failure mode is a session limit makes it adversely correlated with value, since the long, dense sessions are both the most worth recording and the most likely to exhaust the budget before the capture runs. This is the same coupling as the alert path that shared its monitored system's budget in [[connections/alerting-path-must-not-share-the-monitored-systems-budget]], and the mitigation is the same in form: the capture should not compete with the work for the same exhaustible resource.\n\nThe unopened `perch-pull` log deserves separate note because it is a distinct and very cheap failure. The hard part of diagnosing a two-day-old pull failure — knowing which file holds the evidence — was already done and written down; what remained was to read it. An action item of the form \"the answer is at this path, not yet opened\" has essentially no residual cost to resolve and essentially no cost to carry, which is exactly why it carries. The honest annotation is still the right thing to have written: recording that the explanation is unverified prevents a later session from treating a plausible guess about Perch's failure as something that was checked, which is the discipline established in [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]].\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — The sync run whose manifest gap and compiler backlog this session continues.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard and the auto-discovery follow-up this recurrence argues for.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — The largest prior instance of declared-not-discovered manifest coverage.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The deferred-compile warning, now joined by an upstream capture failure.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why these warnings persist until a verified run rather than an intended fix.\n- [[concepts/git-fetch-before-declaring-clean]] — The same skill's other way of producing an unwarranted clean verdict.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — Why per-tool green status does not compose into coverage.\n- [[connections/alerting-path-must-not-share-the-monitored-systems-budget]] — The session-limit flush failure as a budget-coupling instance.\n- [[concepts/verify-the-diagnosis-in-a-received-report]] — The discipline of marking an explanation as unchecked until it is checked.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46) action items: investigate `perch-pull` failures from 9/26–9/27 with the log at `~/DEV/PlayerProfiles_FB_03-28-2026/logs/last_run.json` noted as not yet opened; `SecondBrain` still behind GitHub and not covered by `/dev-sync`, needing a separate pull; `RunningTheRoom` missing from the daily-backup manifest (`manifest-unmanaged`), recurring since 9/24; memory compiler backlogged a few logs with a flush having failed 9/27 on a session limit.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Connection: Classify the Conflict by the Artifact's Kind, Not by Which Machine Is Newer\"\nconnects:\n  - \"concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge\"\n  - \"concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge\"\n  - \"concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage\"\n  - \"concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts\"\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-29\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Connection: Classify the Conflict by the Artifact's Kind, Not by Which Machine Is Newer\n\n## The Connection\n\nFour cross-machine conflict investigations in this workspace have now produced four different correct resolutions, and in every case the resolution was determined by what the conflicting file *is* rather than by which machine wrote it last. A transform artifact gets discarded; an append-only log gets unioned; an accumulating record gets merged by key; a set keyed by identity gets deduplicated. The 2026-09-29 `RESEARCH.md` duplicate supplies the fourth kind and completes the taxonomy.\n\n## Key Insight\n\nEvery sync tool presents a conflict in the same vocabulary — two versions, pick one — and that vocabulary is wrong for most of the files it is applied to. Byte-level divergence is the only thing the tool can measure; what to do about it is a semantic question the tool cannot see. The consequence is that *no* blanket resolution rule is safe, including the two most tempting ones: \"newest wins\" destroys rows in an accumulating log, and \"keep both\" duplicates entries in an index. Classification must therefore come before resolution, and the classification is cheap — it is a property of the file's role that the operator already knows.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- **Transform artifact → discard, and fix the generator.** Roughly 200 `.from-*` files were traced to a Windows save command that re-encoded text and prepended a BOM; none represented a content difference at all, and `portfolio.json` un-mangled matched on all 9 supposedly-missing updates ([[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]). Twenty-six of thirty-six earlier conflicts were likewise CRLF-only ([[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]).\n- **Append-only log → union, keep every line.** `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` conflicts resolved identically by hand on 9/16, by a configured merge driver on 9/22, and by stripping only the conflict markers on 9/24 — three independent routes to the same output ([[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]).\n- **Accumulating record → merge by key; newer may be smaller.** `weekly_forecast_log.csv` was *smaller* on the Mac, and a \"Mac wins\" rule would have discarded 324 Windows-only forecast rows dating to 2026-08-26 ([[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]). The same error one layer down appeared as a DuckDB loader replacing where it should have merged, deleting 12,750 Mac-only rows ([[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]]).\n- **Set keyed by identity → deduplicate.** Both machines added a `Flint` entry to `~/DEV/RESEARCH.md`; the resolution kept one line and dropped the other, and a union strategy would have listed the project twice ([[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]]).\n- **Opaque revisable document → hand comparison.** Three `SoftwareTech_Mgmt_Framework` Word/Excalidraw deliverables, with the DRAFT at 1.0 MB on Windows against 0.4 MB on the Mac, were deliberately left for a human rather than resolved by any rule.\n\nTwo corollaries fall out of the evidence. First, the conflict *count* is not the work estimate: 36 collapsed to 4, and ~200 collapsed to 0 real differences — the same frame error as the git-scope miscounts in [[connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate]]. Second, a merge strategy is correct only for the artifact class it was derived from, so the union driver that was the right fix for transcript logs would be a defect if applied to the router index — a reminder that [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] presumes the generator's scope has been bounded to the class it serves.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]]\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]]\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-encoding-mangling-phantom-conflicts]]\n- [[concepts/duckdb-snapshot-merge-by-primary-key]]\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]\n- [[connections/name-and-flag-are-not-the-artifact]]\n- [[connections/git-scope-errors-inflate-the-work-estimate]]\n- [[connections/ground-truth-checks-must-match-the-lying-layer]]\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work.md===\n---\ntitle: \"Commit Recency Is Not Work Recency: Sync Commits Mask the Active Project\"\naliases: [newest commit is a sync commit, CFB later commits were sync only, 13:20 merge group, picking the active project, commit timestamp is not a work signal, classify the commit before ranking it, graphify-out daily job commits, exclude automated repos from pick-up ranking, commit-days ranking, 26 active 115 dormant, new project is not yet active]\ntags: [git, workflow, verification, project-management, gotcha, automation]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-19.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-24.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-24\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# Commit Recency Is Not Work Recency: Sync Commits Mask the Active Project\n\nChoosing which project to work on during the 2026-09-24 evening session started from the obvious signal — the most recent commit across repositories — and that signal was wrong. `CFB_Victory_Model` carried the latest timestamps, but its commits were sync commits produced by automation and cross-machine reconciliation, not authored work. The genuinely newest real work was a 13:20 merge group in KeystoneOS, which was selected instead. The same trap had already been hit five days earlier, on 2026-09-19, when `graphify-out` topped the recency ranking on the strength of an automated daily job and had to be excluded before `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` could be identified as the real pick-up repo. A commit timestamp records when the repository was written to, which is a strictly weaker claim than when a human last worked on it. By 2026-09-29 the ranking had been rebuilt on commit-*days* rather than recency — and still picked the wrong thread, for the opposite reason.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Automation authors commits.** Nightly backups that commit working trees, sync jobs that push reconciled state, scheduled generation jobs, and merge commits from cross-machine pulls all advance the timestamp without representing any human decision.\n- **This inflates exactly the repos with the most machinery around them,** so the repositories with the best automation look the most active — an inversion of the signal's intent. `graphify-out` is generated output with a daily job; `CFB_Victory_Model` is heavily synced.\n- **Ranking by commit-days instead of recency fixes the automation noise** — the 2026-09-29 pass separated 26 active from 115 dormant-and-clean — **but introduces a symmetric error: genuinely new work has not accumulated enough days to qualify.** `Flint` was the correct thread and did not appear on the active list at all.\n- **No commit-derived metric identifies the intended thread.** On 9/29 the \"newest active repo\" heuristic nominated `TypeSafe_Sandbox`; only the user's clarification — that the Drive-triage work belongs to Flint — supplied the right answer.\n- **The fix is to classify the commit before ranking it:** read the message and the diff, and ask whether a human authored the change. A *group* of related commits at one time (the 13:20 merge group) reads as a work session; a lone `sync`, backup, or generation commit does not.\n- **A wrong answer here costs a whole session's direction,** not a single file, because it determines which project gets picked up and which handoff gets read.\n\n## Details\n\nThe underlying error is treating a proxy as the property it stands in for. \"Most recently committed\" is a genuinely useful heuristic for \"most recently worked on\" in a repository that only a human writes to, and it degrades exactly in proportion to how much automated writing happens. In this stack the automated write volume is substantial: the nightly backup commits and pushes working trees, `dev-sync` pulls and merges across the Windows and Mac machines, union merges append to shared logs, and a daily job regenerates `graphify-out` — each producing commits whose timestamps are recent and whose content is nobody's work. The most-automated repository therefore floats to the top of a recency ranking, which is precisely backwards from the intent of the query. The `repo-behind-blocked` failure on 9/24 is a reminder of how much of this traffic there is: the backup's ordinary mode of operation is to author commits in repositories the human has not touched.\n\nThat the same mistake was made twice within a week, on two different repositories and for two different automation causes, is the argument that the heuristic needs replacing rather than remembering. On 9/19 the tell was known in advance — the session explicitly noted that `graphify-out`'s newer commits came from an automated daily job — and the exclusion still had to be made by hand; on 9/24 the same reasoning had to be re-derived for sync commits. A rule applied manually at each use is a rule that will eventually be skipped, and it will be skipped on the evening when the operator is least inclined to read commit messages.\n\nThe 2026-09-29 pass is the instructive follow-up, because the obvious repair was made and the question still came out wrong. Ranking by commit-days — how many distinct days a repo received commits — is a volume measure rather than a recency one, so a single nightly sync commit no longer promotes a repository, and the 26-active / 115-dormant split it produced was recorded as having worked. But volume measures penalise newness by construction: a project created days ago cannot have many commit-days no matter how much attention it is getting, so `Flint` — the genuinely current thread — sat below the active threshold while `TypeSafe_Sandbox`, an older repo, was nominated as the newest active repo. Recency over-weights automation; volume under-weights new work. The two metrics fail in opposite directions on the same question, which is the signal that the question is not answerable from commit history at all. What actually resolved it was asking: the user clarified that the Drive-triage work belonged to Flint. The durable conclusion is that intent needs an explicit record — a current-thread marker, or a `STATUS.md` whose \"Next\" section is read rather than inferred, per [[connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look]] — and that commit metrics should be treated as a candidate list to confirm, never as an answer.\n\nThe remedy for the automation half remains the classify-before-resolving discipline that has repeatedly paid off elsewhere in this system. [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] collapsed thirty-six flagged conflicts to four by asking what *kind* of difference each one was before adjudicating any of them, and [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] generalizes the lesson that a later timestamp says nothing about content when the two versions are not revisions of one another. Applied to commit history, the classification is between authored and mechanical commits, and the cheap tell is shape rather than content: a cluster of commits within a short window, touching related files, with human-written messages, is a work session; an isolated commit with a generated message is not.\n\nA practical corollary is that automated commits should be made identifiable so this classification is mechanical rather than a judgement call each time. Consistent message prefixes for backup, sync, and generation commits, or a distinct committer identity, would let a recency query filter them out directly — the same reasoning that makes greppable `skipped` markers in [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] turn a cutover into a checkable line count. Absent such a convention, every \"what was I last working on?\" question requires reading the commits, and the risk is that the reading is skipped precisely on the evenings when the answer matters most.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — The parent lesson that a later timestamp carries no claim about content.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline, applied there to conflict files and here to commits.\n- [[concepts/repo-behind-blocks-nightly-push]] — The same session's evidence of how much commit traffic automation generates.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] — The sync machinery whose merges and union merges produce recent, non-authored commits.\n- [[concepts/unattended-cross-machine-push-collisions]] — Unattended pushes from the other machine arriving mid-session as further non-authored history.\n- [[concepts/git-sync-status-versus-uncommitted-work]] — A neighbouring git indicator whose narrow predicate is read as a broader operational fact.\n- [[concepts/decommissioned-leg-verified-by-skipped-markers]] — The precedent for making a mechanical marker greppable so a check becomes automatic.\n- [[concepts/git-fetch-before-declaring-clean]] — The other 2026-09-19 sync finding, where the same skill's clean verdicts were unfounded.\n- [[concepts/graphify-incremental-merge]] — The graphify pipeline whose daily job produces the automated commits excluded from the 9/19 ranking.\n- [[concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing]] — The Flint leg that the 9/29 ranking failed to surface and the user's clarification identified.\n- [[connections/write-the-decision-where-each-consumer-will-look]] — Why the intended next thread needs an explicit record rather than an inferred one.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-19.md]] — Morning `/dev-sync` session (09:17): `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` chosen as the pick-up repo on newest real commit, with `graphify-out` explicitly excluded because its newer commits come from an automated daily job rather than work; `graphify-out`'s 10 generated-output files committed rather than left dirty; recorded as the lesson that sorting by commit recency picks automated-job repos over real work and that automation must be filtered before choosing a pick-up repo.\n- [[daily/2026-09-24.md]] — Evening KeystoneOS session (21:25): KeystoneOS selected as the active project because the 13:20 merge group was the newest real work while `CFB_Victory_Model`'s later commits were only sync commits; recorded as the lesson that a newer commit timestamp does not mean newer work and that sync commits masked the genuinely most-recent project.\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46): ranking repos by commit-days rather than recency recorded as having worked (26 active vs 115 dormant-and-clean), but `Flint` identified as genuinely new work that still did not appear on the active list, so the \"newest active repo\" pick alone would have chosen `TypeSafe_Sandbox` — the wrong thread — and Eric's clarification that the Drive-triage work belongs to Flint was required.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===ARTICLE concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge.md===\n---\ntitle: \"DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\"\naliases: [union merge transcripts, TOS_Evidence_Brief missing from manifest, 146 repos walked, Linda Projects space breaks sync step, Tier A does not cross machines, backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22, 22 active 117 dormant, manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom, delete only the conflict markers, keep both sides no from-files, RESEARCH.md duplicate entry]\ntags: [git, sync, backup, macos, memory, workflow, verification]\nsources:\n  - \"daily/2026-09-16.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-22.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-24.md\"\n  - \"daily/2026-09-29.md\"\ncreated: 2026-09-22\nupdated: 2026-10-01\n---\n\n# DEV Sync 2026-09-22: Union-Merge Transcripts, Manifest Non-Discovery, and Cross-Machine Memory\n\nA `/dev-sync` run on 2026-09-22 walked 146 repositories to bring the Mac in line with GitHub: two pulled (`_INBOX`, `DataBase_GPS_Reporting`), one pushed (`CommandCentre`), one cloned (`TOS_Evidence_Brief`, created on Windows 9/21), with 22 active and 117 dormant-and-clean, and no conflicts. Its durable findings concern how recurring cross-machine friction is resolved: a merge strategy for append-only logs, a manifest that does not discover its own new inputs, and the fact that Tier A memory does not travel between machines. The transcript-merge finding was already established on 2026-09-16 and recurred again on 2026-09-24, making it a three-instance pattern; the manifest gap recurred on 9/24 and again on 9/29.\n\n## Key Points\n\n- **Union merge is the right strategy for append-only transcript logs.** Mac/Windows conflicts in `global-memory/transcripts/*.md` resolve cleanly by keeping every line from both machines, producing no `.from-*` files to clean up afterward.\n- **The same resolution was reached independently on 2026-09-16**, where `global-memory` transcript-log conflicts were settled by keeping both sides rather than picking a winner, again with no `.from-*` files created — before the merge strategy was configured.\n- **The manual form is to delete only the conflict markers.** On 2026-09-24 two clashing transcript logs (9/23 and 9/24) were resolved exactly this way — text from both sides kept, only the `<<<<<<<`/`=======`/`>>>>>>>` lines removed — confirming that for this artifact class there is nothing to adjudicate.\n- **The union strategy is class-specific, not general.** On 2026-09-29 a conflict on the router file `~/DEV/RESEARCH.md` was a *duplicate* `Flint` entry added independently by both machines, and the correct resolution was to drop one line — a union would have listed the project twice.\n- **The backup manifest does not auto-discover new repos.** `TOS_Evidence_Brief` — created on Windows the previous day — was absent from `~/.claude/scripts/backup-manifest.json`, which made the 09:05 backup report an error. Adding it fixed the report (prior file at `backup-manifest.json.bak-2026-09-22`). On 2026-09-24 the identical class reappeared as `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom`, and the same entry was still outstanding on 2026-09-29.\n- **Tier A memory does not cross machines.** Durable GPS decisions were therefore written into the repo's own `docs/STATUS.md` *and* `global-memory/MEMORY.md` so the Windows machine would see them.\n- **A folder name containing a space (`DEV/Linda Projects`) broke a sync step again** and had to be redone with the path quoted — a recurrence of the same hazard seen on 2026-09-11.\n- **`compile.py` was deliberately not run.** A compile costs ~$1.28, so clearing the memory-compiler backlog was left as the user's call; the warning persists in `.errors.log` until then, and had grown to 3 logs behind by 2026-09-24.\n\n## Details\n\nThe union-merge finding names a resolution strategy that fits the artifact's semantics rather than a generic \"take one side\" rule. A transcript log is append-only and each machine appends independently, so the two versions are not competing revisions of the same content — they are disjoint additions to a shared record. Under that structure the correct merge keeps every line, and the usual conflict-file machinery (`.from-*` copies awaiting manual adjudication) is pure overhead because there is nothing to adjudicate. The pattern now has three independent confirmations arriving by different routes: a hand resolution on 9/16 that consciously declined to pick a winner, a configured merge driver on 9/22, and a purely mechanical marker-strip on 9/24. That an unaided human judgment, a configured strategy, and a mechanical cleanup all converge on identical output is the strongest available evidence that the strategy matches the artifact rather than the tooling — and it argues that the merge driver should have been configured after the first instance rather than the second. This is the same classify-by-kind-before-resolving discipline applied in [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]], where treating accumulating records as revisions would have discarded 324 unique forecast-log rows, and the accumulate-versus-supersede distinction generalized in [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]]. Notably, the 9/22 PFF loader bug was the identical error one layer down — a replace where a merge was required.\n\nThe 2026-09-29 `RESEARCH.md` conflict is the boundary case that keeps the union strategy honest. Both machines had independently added a router entry for the new `Flint` project, so the conflict was concurrent agreement rather than disagreement — and the correct resolution was to keep one line and drop the other, because a router index is a set keyed by project identity rather than an append-only sequence. Applying the transcript strategy there would have produced a doubled entry. The practical rule, developed in [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]] and synthesised across all four observed artifact classes in [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]], is that a merge strategy is correct only for the class it was derived from, and that a conflict on the `~/DEV` router files should be read as an expected duplicate rather than investigated as a mangled file.\n\nThe manifest gap is a recurrence rather than a novelty, and its recurrence is the point. A repo created on one machine does not announce itself to a backup manifest maintained on another; the manifest's coverage is declared, not discovered, so a new repo is invisible to it until a human adds a line. The 09:05 backup error was in this case an honest signal — the manifest check did fire — but the general hazard is the one catalogued in [[connections/silent-omission-defeats-per-stage-success]] and [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]]: every managed item succeeds while the unmanaged item is never counted. Prior instances at [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] and [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]], the `Claude outputs` complaint resolved on 9/16 by relocation rather than by a manifest entry ([[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]]), the `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` entry two days after this run, and that same entry still outstanding a further five days later ([[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]]), put the count well past the threshold at which — by the reasoning in [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — the fix belongs in the discovery mechanism rather than in yet another manual manifest edit. Each individual fix is a one-line change, which is precisely why the structural fix keeps getting deferred.\n\nThe Tier A observation is a routing constraint with immediate practical force. Tier A memory is local to the machine that wrote it, so any decision that a second machine must honor — here, that the Mac owns the pulls and the Windows GPS writer is stopped — cannot live there alone. Writing it into the repository's `docs/STATUS.md` puts it in a location git already replicates, and mirroring it into `global-memory/MEMORY.md` reaches the synced memory surface; between them the decision arrives on the other machine by two independent paths. This extends the content-to-tier routing matrix in [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] with a cross-machine axis, and matches the working-machine-authors-the-fix pattern in [[concepts/fix-from-the-working-machine-handoff]]. Finally, the deliberate refusal to run `compile.py` is a recorded non-action rather than an oversight: a ~$1.28 spend is a user decision, so the backlog warning was left standing in `.errors.log` with the command documented in the compiler's README — consistent with treating `.errors.log` as an escalation channel that clears only on a verified clean run.\n\n## Related Concepts\n\n- [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]] — The 2026-09-29 router conflict showing that the union strategy is class-specific.\n- [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]] — The four-class taxonomy this run's union merge anchors.\n- [[concepts/cross-machine-conflict-file-triage]] — The prior conflict triage that established classifying by kind before resolving; union merge is the resolution for the accumulating-record kind.\n- [[connections/newer-is-not-more-complete]] — Why an append-only log must be merged rather than overwritten.\n- [[concepts/backup-manifest-coverage-gap-2026-09-09]] — An earlier instance of the same declared-not-discovered manifest gap.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-manifest-default-deny]] — The fail-closed manifest guard designed against exactly this omission.\n- [[concepts/dev-folder-is-not-a-document-store]] — The 2026-09-16 manifest complaint resolved by relocating the folder instead of widening coverage.\n- [[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]] — The same manifest entry and compiler backlog still outstanding a week later.\n- [[connections/aggregate-success-hides-a-shrunken-work-set]] — The general shape: an unlisted item cannot fail a check it was never part of.\n- [[concepts/dev-sync-false-missing-repos]] — The 2026-09-11 sync where `Linda Projects`' embedded space first broke the parallel fetch loop.\n- [[concepts/claude-code-memory-architecture]] — The tier-routing matrix this session extends with a cross-machine constraint.\n- [[concepts/compiler-backlog-hook-notice]] — The backlog warning deliberately left in `.errors.log`, at 3 logs behind by 2026-09-24.\n- [[concepts/clear-the-error-log-only-after-a-clean-run]] — Why the warning stays until a verified clean compile, not until the fix is merely intended.\n- [[concepts/mac-owned-data-pulls-sleep-lateness]] — The GPS decisions whose cross-machine visibility motivated the dual write.\n- [[concepts/mac-evening-machine-schedule-split]] — The 2026-09-24 session in which both the union-merge and manifest-gap findings recurred.\n- [[connections/fix-the-generator-not-the-artifacts]] — The recurrence argument for moving the manifest fix into discovery.\n\n## Sources\n\n- [[daily/2026-09-16.md]] — End-of-day wrap session (backfilled 2026-09-19 12:24): `global-memory` transcript-log conflicts resolved by keeping both sides rather than picking a winner, with no `.from-*` files created — the first of three instances of the union-merge resolution.\n- [[daily/2026-09-22.md]] — Sync and closeout session (17:25): `/dev-sync` walking 146 repos with `_INBOX` and `DataBase_GPS_Reporting` pulled, `CommandCentre` pushed, `TOS_Evidence_Brief` cloned, 22 active / 117 dormant / no conflicts; union merge resolving Mac/Windows transcript-log conflicts without `.from-*` files; `TOS_Evidence_Brief` missing from `backup-manifest.json` causing the 09:05 backup error, fixed with a `.bak-2026-09-22` backup; `DEV/Linda Projects` space breaking a sync step; Tier A memory not crossing machines, resolved by writing to `docs/STATUS.md` plus `global-memory/MEMORY.md`; `compile.py` (~$1.28) deliberately left as the user's call.\n- [[daily/2026-09-24.md]] — Evening KeystoneOS closeout (21:25): two clashing transcript logs (9/23, 9/24) resolved by keeping text from both sides and deleting only the conflict markers; `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` reported by the backup log and queued as an action item; memory compiler 3 logs behind with a manual `compile.py` run still outstanding.\n- [[daily/2026-09-29.md]] — Memory maintenance session (08:46): router conflict on `RESEARCH.md` where both machines had added a `Flint` entry, resolved by hand by dropping the Mac's duplicate line with no `.from-*` files created and recorded as the expected shape of a `RESEARCH.md` merge; `RunningTheRoom` still missing from the daily-backup manifest, recurring since 9/24; memory compiler still backlogged a few logs.\n\n===END ARTICLE===\n\n===INDEX===\n| [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]] | Both machines added the same `Flint` router line to `RESEARCH.md`; a router index is a set keyed by identity, so the correct merge is deduplicate — not union, not newest-wins | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/blind-answer-key-before-first-api-call]] | Jev triage pilot gated on writing and committing Claude's blind answer key before any API call; pre-registration standing in for an absent reviewer, with the sample pinning the population | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing]] | Flint's books/OCR/audio are Windows-only, but a committed 200-file `sample.csv` plus a mounted Drive made the triage leg Mac-runnable; credentials remain per-machine (untested API key) | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]] | `SecondBrain` outside `/dev-sync`'s scope, `manifest-unmanaged:RunningTheRoom` recurring since 9/24, a 9/27 flush failing on a session limit, and a located-but-unopened `perch-pull` log | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]] | Four cross-machine conflicts, four correct resolutions — discard, union, merge-by-key, deduplicate — each set by what the file is, so no blanket rule is safe | 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work]] | Adds the 2026-09-29 commit-days ranking, which fixed the automation noise but under-weighted new work (`Flint` absent from 26 active), showing commit metrics cannot name the intended thread | 2026-09-19.md, 2026-09-24.md, 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n| [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]] | Adds the 2026-09-29 `RESEARCH.md` duplicate-entry conflict as the boundary case showing union merge is class-specific, plus the still-outstanding `RunningTheRoom` manifest gap | 2026-09-16.md, 2026-09-22.md, 2026-09-24.md, 2026-09-29.md | 2026-10-01 |\n===END INDEX===\n\n===LOG===\n## [2026-10-01T06:03:55-05:00] compile | 2026-09-29.md\n- Source: daily/2026-09-29.md\n- Articles created: [[concepts/router-index-duplicate-entry-merge]], [[concepts/blind-answer-key-before-first-api-call]], [[concepts/committed-sample-enables-cross-machine-testing]], [[concepts/carried-forward-maintenance-residue-2026-09-29]], [[connections/classify-the-conflict-by-the-artifacts-kind]]\n- Articles updated: [[concepts/sync-commits-mask-most-recent-work]], [[concepts/dev-sync-2026-09-22-manifest-and-union-merge]]\n===END LOG==="}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89567,"cache_read_input_tokens":9707,"output_tokens":24713,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4950},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89567,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":24713,"cache_read_input_tokens":9707,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89567,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89567},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfbQWigVrgs5E4LFUiJPa","type":"assistant","uuid":"00076569-28e7-48cf-b22f-ef9e70e9a384","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:08:52.026Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cc7a7290-35b1-45f8-8a39-c4c00a47afa6","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"00076569-28e7-48cf-b22f-ef9e70e9a384","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"deferred_tools_record","entries":[{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__batch","description":"Create a doc, or apply several operations to one doc atomically.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"batch":{"type":"array"},"container":{"type":"object","properties":{"kind":{"type":"string"},"id":{"type":"string"},"create":{"type":"object"}},"required":["kind"]},"verbose":{"type":"boolean"},"opId":{"type":"string"}}},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__guide","description":"Docs guides: topic.instructions repeats the server instructions. Read it only if your client dropped them. Also topic.<name>, refusal.<code>. After a doc's birth → [\"topic.index\"].","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"items":{"type":"array","description":"topic.<name> (instructions, index, editing, tabs, comments, charts, chart-definition, diagram, uploads, sharing, skill) or refusal.<code>; several per call is fine."}}},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__update","description":"Edit a tab's contents, rename a doc or tab, or change a stored value.","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"ref":{"type":"object","properties":{"object":{"type":"string","enum":["project","file","node","utterance","enum"]},"id":{"type":"string"}},"required":["object","id"]},"engine":{"type":"string"},"payload":{"anyOf":[{"type":"object"},{"type":"string"}]},"container":{"type":"object","properties":{"kind":{"type":"string"},"id":{"type":"string"},"version":{"type":"string"}},"required":["kind","id"]},"verbose":{"type":"boolean"},"opId":{"type":"string"},"answering":{"type":"string","maxLength":64}},"required":["ref","payload"]},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Firecrawl__firecrawl_scrape","description":"\nScrape one URL and return its content, or execute catalogued Alexandria capabilities. URL mode returns markdown by default, or HTML, links, screenshots, branding data, a targeted answer, or JSON matching a supplied schema, plus page metadata. Firecrawl may serve recently indexed content; set `maxAge: 0` for a live fetch. A successful response does not by itself confirm the page is still current. Browser actions can change the live page when interactive actions are enabled, and a named browser profile can load saved session data and overwrite its stored state.\n\n`firecrawl_search` with `sources` unset and `firecrawl_find_tools` can discover providers for the same fields across several pages; a matching Alexandria provider returns typed records in one call.\n\nAlexandria mode: `alexandria` selects catalogued capability execution and is mutually exclusive with `url`. Alexandria execution is billed at the capability's listed price and needs a team with Alexandria enabled.\n","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"url":{"type":"string","format":"uri"},"formats":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["markdown","html","rawHtml","screenshot","links","summary","changeTracking","branding","json","query","audio"]}},"jsonOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"prompt":{"type":"string"},"schema":{"type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false},"queryOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"prompt":{"type":"string","maxLength":10000},"mode":{"default":"freeform","type":"string","enum":["directQuote","freeform"]}},"required":["prompt"],"additionalProperties":false},"screenshotOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"fullPage":{"type":"boolean"},"quality":{"type":"number"},"viewport":{"type":"object","properties":{"width":{"type":"number"},"height":{"type":"number"}},"required":["width","height"],"additionalProperties":false}},"additionalProperties":false},"parsers":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["pdf"]}},"pdfOptions":{"type":"object","properties":{"maxPages":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":10000}},"additionalProperties":false},"onlyMainContent":{"type":"boolean"},"redactPII":{"type":"boolean"},"includeTags":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"excludeTags":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}},"waitFor":{"type":"number"},"mobile":{"type":"boolean"},"skipTlsVerification":{"type":"boolean"},"removeBase64Images":{"type":"boolean"},"location":{"type":"object","properties":{"country":{"type":"string"},"languages":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string"}}},"additionalProperties":false},"storeInCache":{"type":"boolean"},"zeroDataRetention":{"type":"boolean"},"maxAge":{"type":"number"},"lockdown":{"type":"boolean"},"proxy":{"type":"string","enum":["basic","stealth","enhanced","auto"]},"profile":{"type":"object","properties":{"name":{"type":"string"},"saveChanges":{"type":"boolean"}},"required":["name"],"additionalProperties":false},"timeout":{"description":"Execution timeout in milliseconds.","type":"integer","exclusiveMinimum":0,"maximum":9007199254740991},"requestId":{"description":"Idempotency key bound to one Alexandria execution payload. Generated when omitted and returned with the result.","type":"string","pattern":"^[A-Za-z0-9._:-]{1,128}$"},"alexandria":{"description":"Catalogued Alexandria capability invocation, mutually exclusive with url. One {provider, capability, options} object or an array of 1-10, with contracts available through firecrawl_search or firecrawl_find_tools. Each call may include version to pin a published workflow; omitting it uses latest. Only requestId and timeout are supported alongside alexandria. The selected contract marks required inputs and any requiresOneOf groups (at least one member per group); it may include example.request/example.response and response.key (which may differ from records). Where pagination is declared, its fields govern paging with the same filters; catalogue next is separate from provider pagination. Returns per-capability results in data.alexandria with data, records, or an error with a code; individual capabilities can fail even when the outer response succeeds. Requires an API key on a team with Alexandria enabled. Some providers require accepted terms; blocked requests return the applicable requirements.","anyOf":[{"type":"object","properties":{"provider":{"description":"Provider slug, e.g. \"fred\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"capability":{"description":"Capability address as returned by search or discover, e.g. \"series/observations\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"version":{"description":"Optional published workflow version. Omit to use the latest version.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"options":{"description":"Capability options as declared by its contract.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["provider","capability"]},{"minItems":1,"maxItems":10,"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","properties":{"provider":{"description":"Provider slug, e.g. \"fred\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"capability":{"description":"Capability address as returned by search or discover, e.g. \"series/observations\".","type":"string","minLength":1},"version":{"description":"Optional published workflow version. Omit to use the latest version.","type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":128},"options":{"description":"Capability options as declared by its contract.","type":"object","propertyNames":{"type":"string"},"additionalProperties":{}}},"required":["provider","capability"]}}]},"toolDetail":{"description":"URL mode only: domain discovery detail, summary by default; compact returns provider/capability/description, full includes contracts. Ignored with alexandria.","type":"string","enum":["compact","summary","full"]},"domainTools":{"description":"URL mode only: include domain-matched Alexandria tools for the page in tools on the returned document. Ignored with alexandria.","type":"boolean"}},"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true},{"name":"mcp__claude_ai_Firecrawl__firecrawl_search","description":"\nSearch web and specialized indexes, returning ranked results with query-relevant highlights. Each web result is a title, URL, and description. Operators include quoted phrases, `-term`, `site:host`, `inurl:term`, `intitle:term`, and `related:host`; the set is non-exhaustive. `includeDomains` and `excludeDomains` are mutually exclusive hostname filters; categories limit result types to `research`, `pdf`, or `developer`.\n\nFor a programming question, add `categories: [\"developer\"]`. It searches an index of public repositories, GitHub issues, merged pull requests, repository READMEs, and code documentation, and returns the results in `data.web` with `category: \"developer\"`.\n\nAuthenticated search combines web results, semantic tool summaries and domain matches. Use sources: [\"alexandria\"] for semantic tools only. A search with sources: [\"web\"] omits semantic provider discovery; domainTools: true can still return website-matched tools. Web-only results use domainTools: false. Tool matches describe available structured-data capabilities, not executed data. toolDetail: \"compact\" (default) returns only provider, capability and description; \"summary\" adds metadata; \"full\" includes their input and output contracts. firecrawl_scrape with an alexandria body executes a selected capability; firecrawl_find_tools provides catalogue browsing and full contracts.\n\nReturns result groups in `data` and an operation `id`.\n","input_schema":{"type":"object","properties":{"toolDetail":{"description":"Compact by default. Compact returns only provider, capability and description; full includes contracts. Inspect selected compact tools with firecrawl_find_tools providers and capabilities.","type":"string","enum":["compact","summary","full"]},"query":{"description":"Query for web and semantic tool discovery. Operators include quoted phrases, `-term`, `site:host`, `inurl:term`, `intitle:term`, and `related:host`; the set is non-exhaustive. Catalogue browsing is available through firecrawl_find_tools.","type":"string","minLength":1},"domainTools":{"description":"Include domain-matched tools for result URLs. Defaults to true when Alexandria is combined with web, news or images; semantic-only search leaves domain matching off.","type":"boolean"},"highlights":{"description":"Return query-relevant page excerpts for web and news results when available (default). Highlights appear in web `description` and news `snippet`; otherwise, original snippets are returned. Set to false to keep the original search snippets.","type":"boolean"},"limit":{"type":"integer","minimum":1,"maximum":100},"tbs":{"type":"string"},"filter":{"type":"string"},"location":{"type":"string"},"includeDomains":{"description":"Hostnames to restrict results to. Mutually exclusive with excludeDomains.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":253,"pattern":"^(?:[a-z0-9](?:[a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?\\.)+[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9]$"}},"excludeDomains":{"description":"Hostnames to leave out of results. Mutually exclusive with includeDomains.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","minLength":1,"maxLength":253,"pattern":"^(?:[a-z0-9](?:[a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?\\.)+[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9]$"}},"sources":{"description":"Search sources; authenticated sessions default to web + alexandria, keyless sessions to web only. A search with sources: [\"web\"] omits semantic provider discovery; domainTools: true can still return website-matched tools. Web-only results use domainTools: false. Use [\"alexandria\"] alone for provider discovery without web results.","type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string","enum":["web","images","news","alexandria","exchange"]},{"type":"object","properties":{"type":{"type":"string","enum":["web","images","news","alexandria","exchange"]}},"required":["type"],"additionalProperties":false}]}},"categories":{"description":"Limit results to specific source types. `research` restricts ordinary web results to research-affiliated websites and returns page snippets, which is separate from the `firecrawl_research_*` tools that search paper abstracts and full text across biomedical (PubMed, bioRxiv, medRxiv) and arXiv literature; `pdf` searches PDF results; `developer` searches an index built for coding agents over public repositories, GitHub issues, merged pull requests, repository READMEs, and code documentation. `developer` returns hits in `data.web` with `category: \"developer\"`; the other categories also filter `data.web`.","type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["research","pdf","developer"]}},"enterprise":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"string","enum":["default","anon","zdr"]}}},"required":["query"],"$schema":"http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#","additionalProperties":false},"eager_input_streaming":true,"defer_loading":true}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"943ca358-db7e-4bbb-8b03-6e3a8ac7c035","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:08:52.205Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"sdk-py","cwd":"/Users/kristinarash/.claude/claude-memory-compiler","sessionId":"cc7a7290-35b1-45f8-8a39-c4c00a47afa6","version":"2.1.259","gitBranch":"main"}
